-- Study guides rewrite (2026-10-23): the 7 original guides rewritten in depth
-- with sources, the owner's CSCA registration guide updated and moved to a
-- short slug, and 4 new guides (CSCA overview, documents & attestation,
-- foreign scholarships hub, 2027 intakes). Only the English text ($.en) of
-- existing guides is replaced — their zh/si/ta translations are left as they
-- are (to be re-translated later). Re-runnable: updates are idempotent and
-- inserts are skipped when the slug already exists.
--
-- No USE statement -- see Database/tools/apply-migrations.ps1 / ENVIRONMENTS.md.

-- CSCA registration guide: short slug (the old one was never indexed — it
-- 404'd live; root web.config 301s the old URL to the new one).
IF EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM syst.Guide WHERE Slug = 'how-to-register-for-the-csca-exam-a-step-by-step-guide-for-international-students')
   AND NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM syst.Guide WHERE Slug = 'csca-exam-registration')
    UPDATE syst.Guide SET Slug = 'csca-exam-registration' WHERE Slug = 'how-to-register-for-the-csca-exam-a-step-by-step-guide-for-international-students'
GO

UPDATE syst.Guide SET ContentJSON = JSON_MODIFY(ContentJSON, '$.en', JSON_QUERY(N'{"title":"Study in China from Sri Lanka: The Complete 2027 Guide","seoTitle":"Study in China from Sri Lanka: 2027 Guide | Proton","description":"Study in China from Sri Lanka: programmes, A/L entry rules, CSCA and HSK, scholarships, visa and 2027 intakes, step by step. See what fits you.","bodyHtml":"<p><strong>In short:</strong> You can study in China from Sri Lanka at every level, from a one-year Chinese language course to a bachelor''s degree, MBBS, master''s or PhD, and many programmes are taught fully in English. Most Sri Lankan students apply with their A/L results (or a degree for postgraduate study), choose between a scholarship and self-funding, sit the CSCA test if they are applying for a scholarship-funded bachelor''s place, and then get an X1 student visa from Colombo.</p>\n<p>This guide is the starting point for Sri Lankan students and parents asking \"can I study in China?\" It walks through the whole decision in order and links to a detailed guide for each step, so you can read only the parts you need.</p>\n\n<h2>Why do Sri Lankan students choose to study in China?</h2>\n<p>Sri Lankan students choose China for three practical reasons: large, well-funded public universities; a wide choice of English-taught programmes; and tuition that is usually lower than at comparable universities in the UK or Australia. Generous scholarships, especially the Chinese Government Scholarship, make China one of the few destinations where a fully funded degree is realistic.</p>\n<p><strong>Tuition is published and moderate.</strong> Chinese universities publish their international student fees. As one example, Tsinghua University lists undergraduate tuition of CNY 26,000 a year for economics, finance, management and law, CNY 30,000 for its Chinese-English engineering and science programmes and CNY 40,000 for arts (fees as listed on Tsinghua''s admissions site, accessed October 2026). Tsinghua is one of China''s most expensive universities, and many others charge less. A full year-by-year budget, including rent, food and insurance, is in our <a href=\"/guides/cost-of-studying-in-china-for-sri-lankans\">cost of studying in China guide</a>.</p>\n<p><strong>Scholarships are real and substantial.</strong> A full Chinese Government Scholarship covers tuition, a university dormitory place, comprehensive medical insurance and a monthly stipend, listed by the China Scholarship Council as CNY 2,500 for undergraduates, CNY 3,000 for master''s students and CNY 3,500 for doctoral students. In August 2025, 30 Sri Lankan students received Chinese Government Scholarships for the 2025–2026 academic year, according to Ada Derana.</p>\n<p><strong>Career value.</strong> Sri Lanka and China have close trade, infrastructure and tourism links, so graduates who know the Chinese system and some Mandarin have an extra advantage. That benefit is real but hard to measure, so treat it as a bonus rather than the main reason to go.</p>\n\n<h2>What can I study in China? Programme levels explained</h2>\n<p>Chinese universities accept international students at six main levels. The right level depends on your current qualification: A/L students usually start with a bachelor''s degree or a foundation year, and graduates go on to a master''s or PhD. Chinese language courses suit anyone who wants to learn Mandarin first.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Typical length</th><th>Who it suits</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Foundation / preparatory year</td><td>1 year</td><td>A/L students who need extra Chinese or subject preparation before a Chinese-taught degree</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Chinese language programme</td><td>1 semester to 2 years</td><td>Anyone learning Mandarin, often towards the HSK level a degree requires</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bachelor''s degree</td><td>Usually 4 years</td><td>A/L students: engineering, IT, business, sciences and more</td></tr>\n<tr><td>MBBS / clinical medicine</td><td>Usually 6 years including internship</td><td>Bio-stream A/L students who want to become doctors</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Master''s degree</td><td>2–3 years</td><td>Graduates with a recognised bachelor''s degree</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PhD</td><td>3–4 years or more</td><td>Master''s graduates with a research proposal</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Programme lengths vary by university and subject, so check the exact duration on the programme page before you apply.</p>\n\n<h2>What are the entry requirements to study in China from Sri Lanka?</h2>\n<p>Entry requirements depend on the level and the teaching language. For a bachelor''s degree, Sri Lankan applicants normally need G.C.E. A/L results, a valid passport and a medical form, plus proof of English for English-taught programmes or an HSK certificate for Chinese-taught programmes. Postgraduate applicants need a recognised degree, recommendation letters and a study plan.</p>\n<h3>Bachelor''s degrees and A/L results</h3>\n<p>Chinese universities look at your A/L subjects as well as your grades. Science, engineering and medicine programmes expect Maths- or Bio-stream subjects; business and humanities programmes accept a wider range. There is no single national Z-score cut-off: each university sets its own standard, and competitive universities and scholarship places expect strong results. If you are still deciding what to do with your results, read our guide to <a href=\"/guides/what-to-do-after-al-sri-lanka\">options after A/L in Sri Lanka</a>.</p>\n<p>Example (hypothetical): a Maths-stream student with three B passes who wants civil engineering in English would shortlist English-taught programmes at several universities, prepare proof of English and, if applying for a Chinese Government Scholarship, register for the CSCA Mathematics and Physics papers.</p>\n<h3>English proof or HSK?</h3>\n<p>The teaching language decides the language test. English-taught programmes usually accept IELTS, TOEFL or a letter confirming that your previous education was in English; each university sets its own rules. Chinese-taught programmes ask for an HSK certificate, commonly HSK 4 or above for bachelor''s study, though the level varies by university and subject. Our <a href=\"/guides/learn-chinese-hsk-sri-lanka\">HSK and learning Chinese in Sri Lanka guide</a> explains the levels and where to sit the test locally.</p>\n<h3>The new CSCA test for undergraduate admission</h3>\n<p>The China Scholastic Competency Assessment (CSCA) is a new entrance test for international students applying to bachelor''s programmes in China. According to the Shanghai government''s CSCA FAQ, the CSCA is mandatory for undergraduate applicants at Chinese Government Scholarship universities for the 2026 and 2027 academic years, and self-funded applicants may also take it to strengthen their application.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mathematics</strong> is required for every programme.</li>\n<li><strong>Physics or Chemistry:</strong> applicants to science, engineering, agriculture and medicine must take at least one.</li>\n<li><strong>Professional Chinese</strong> (Humanities or STEM version) is for Chinese-taught programmes; applicants to fully English-taught programmes are exempt from this paper only.</li>\n<li>Maths, Physics and Chemistry can be taken in English or Chinese.</li>\n<li>The fee is CNY 450 for one subject or CNY 700 for two or more, and the test can be taken at a test centre or online from home with live proctoring.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Each university decides which CSCA subjects it requires for each major, so check the admission guide of every university on your list. Read our <a href=\"/guides/csca-exam-sri-lanka\">CSCA exam guide for Sri Lankan students</a> for the full picture, and the <a href=\"/guides/csca-exam-registration\">CSCA registration guide</a> for step-by-step sign-up on the official csca.cn site.</p>\n<h3>Master''s and PhD</h3>\n<p>Postgraduate applicants need a bachelor''s degree (for a master''s) or a master''s degree (for a PhD), academic transcripts, usually two recommendation letters from lecturers, and a study plan or research proposal. The CSCA does not apply to postgraduate admission. PhD applicants often improve their chances by contacting a potential supervisor before applying.</p>\n\n<h2>How do I study in China from Sri Lanka? The decision flow, step by step</h2>\n<p>Studying in China from Sri Lanka follows one clear order: match your A/L stream to a programme, decide how you will pay, sit the right tests, apply, get your visa and register on arrival. Doing these steps in the wrong order is the most common cause of missed deadlines.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Start from your A/L stream.</strong> Bio-stream students can consider MBBS, pharmacy and life sciences; Maths-stream students engineering, IT and physical sciences; Commerce and Arts students business, economics, languages and social sciences.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose the programme and teaching language.</strong> English-taught or Chinese-taught decides whether you need English proof or HSK.</li>\n<li><strong>Choose scholarship or self-funded.</strong> Scholarship routes have earlier deadlines and, for bachelor''s places, need the CSCA. Self-funded routes are more flexible.</li>\n<li><strong>Sit the CSCA and/or HSK.</strong> Book early, because test sessions fill and results take time to reach universities.</li>\n<li><strong>Prepare documents and apply.</strong> Apply to the university and, where relevant, through the scholarship portal.</li>\n<li><strong>Receive your admission letter and JW201 or JW202 form,</strong> then apply for your X1 or X2 student visa in Colombo.</li>\n<li><strong>Travel, register at the university and apply for your residence permit</strong> within 30 days of arrival if you hold an X1 visa.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>When should I apply? March and September 2027 intakes</h2>\n<p>Chinese universities have two intakes: September, the main intake for degree programmes, and March, used mainly for language courses and some degree programmes. For September 2027, scholarship applicants should expect to apply in the first months of 2027; self-funded applicants can often apply later. Exact deadlines differ by university.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>March 2027 intake</th><th>September 2027 intake</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Choose programme; book CSCA, HSK or IELTS</td><td>Now (October 2026)</td><td>Late 2026</td></tr>\n<tr><td>University applications</td><td>Usually late 2026</td><td>Usually late 2026 to spring 2027</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Scholarship deadlines</td><td>Few scholarships start in March</td><td>Commonly early to mid 2027</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Admission letter and JW form</td><td>Usually December 2026 to January 2027</td><td>Usually June to July 2027</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Visa application in Colombo</td><td>January to February 2027</td><td>July to August 2027</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arrival and registration</td><td>Late February to March 2027</td><td>Late August to September 2027</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>These are typical windows, not fixed dates. Check each university''s admission page, and see our <a href=\"/guides/china-university-intakes-and-deadlines\">China university intakes and deadlines 2027 guide</a> for confirmed dates.</p>\n\n<h2>How can I pay to study in China? Scholarships and self-funding</h2>\n<p>You can study in China on a full scholarship, a partial scholarship or fully self-funded. The Chinese Government Scholarship (CSC) is the largest scheme and is open to Sri Lankans through two routes: the bilateral route, announced in Sri Lanka by the Ministry of Education, and the university route, where you apply directly to a Chinese university.</p>\n<p>Beyond CSC, there are provincial, city and university scholarships, often covering full or partial tuition. Our <a href=\"/guides/chinese-government-scholarship-sri-lanka\">Chinese Government Scholarship guide for Sri Lankans</a> explains eligibility, the two routes and how to write a strong study plan. If you are comparing China with other countries, the <a href=\"/guides/foreign-scholarships-for-sri-lankan-students\">foreign scholarships for Sri Lankan students hub</a> covers scholarships worldwide.</p>\n<p>Self-funded students pay tuition, accommodation, insurance and living costs. Living costs in cities such as Qingdao, Harbin or Chengdu are usually lower than in Beijing or Shanghai. The <a href=\"/guides/cost-of-studying-in-china-for-sri-lankans\">China cost guide for Sri Lankans</a> has a full breakdown.</p>\n\n<h2>Which documents do I need to study in China?</h2>\n<p>Most applications ask for a passport, A/L or degree certificates and transcripts, passport photos, a study plan, recommendation letters, the Foreigner Physical Examination Form, a police clearance report and a language certificate. Certificates often need certified copies or attestation, and requirements differ by university and scholarship.</p>\n<p>The <a href=\"/guides/documents-for-studying-in-china-sri-lanka\">documents for studying in China guide</a> lists each document, how to get a Sri Lankan police clearance and how attestation works.</p>\n\n<h2>How do I get a China student visa from Sri Lanka?</h2>\n<p>Sri Lankan students apply for a Chinese student visa in Colombo after receiving their admission letter and JW201 or JW202 form. An X1 visa is for study longer than 180 days and an X2 visa for shorter courses. X1 holders must apply for a residence permit at the local public security bureau within 30 days of arriving in China.</p>\n<p>Wuhan University, for example, warns that late residence permit applicants face fines, and that students can apply only after registering at the university. Fees, appointment steps and the full checklist are in our <a href=\"/guides/china-student-visa-sri-lanka\">China student visa guide for Sri Lankans</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Can I study MBBS in China from Sri Lanka?</h2>\n<p>Yes. Many Chinese universities offer English-taught MBBS programmes, usually six years including internship. The key point for Sri Lankans is recognition: to practise in Sri Lanka you must graduate from an institution recognised by the Sri Lanka Medical Council (SLMC) and pass the Examination for Registration to Practise Medicine (ERPM).</p>\n<p>Check the SLMC recognised institutions list for your exact university and programme before you pay any fees. Our <a href=\"/guides/mbbs-in-china-sri-lanka\">MBBS in China guide for Sri Lankans</a> covers costs, entry requirements and recognition in detail.</p>\n\n<h2>How do I choose a university and city in China?</h2>\n<p>Choose a university by programme first, then by city. Look for a programme in your teaching language that fits your A/L stream, a university that accepts scholarship students if you need funding, and a city whose climate and cost of living suit you. Northern cities such as Harbin are very cold in winter; southern cities are hot and humid in summer.</p>\n<p>Proton works with 17 Chinese universities, including Tsinghua University and Beihang University in Beijing, Fudan University and Shanghai Jiao Tong University in Shanghai, Zhejiang University in Hangzhou, Harbin Institute of Technology, and Ocean University of China and China University of Petroleum in Qingdao, where Proton has its China office. The full list is on our <a href=\"/services/education\">study in China service page</a>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Beijing and Shanghai:</strong> top-ranked universities and the highest living costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Qingdao and Dalian:</strong> coastal cities with more moderate costs and strong engineering and marine science.</li>\n<li><strong>Wuhan, Chengdu, Xi''an, Nanjing and Changchun:</strong> large university cities with lower living costs.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>What is life in China like for Sri Lankan students?</h2>\n<p>Most international students live in their university''s international student dormitory, at least in the first year. Daily life runs on mobile payments, mainly Alipay and WeChat Pay, so set these up soon after arrival. Chinese campuses and cities are generally safe, but keep your passport and residence permit secure and follow local registration rules.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Housing:</strong> university dormitories are usually the cheapest and simplest option; renting off campus means registering your address with the local police.</li>\n<li><strong>Payments:</strong> Alipay and WeChat Pay are used almost everywhere, from canteens to metro tickets.</li>\n<li><strong>Food:</strong> campus canteens are cheap, and many universities have a halal canteen.</li>\n<li><strong>Insurance:</strong> health insurance is compulsory for international students and is often arranged through the university.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Register with the Sri Lankan Embassy in Beijing.</strong> In January 2025 the Sri Lankan Embassy in China asked students who applied to Chinese universities on their own, rather than through government scholarships, to share their details so the embassy can help them in emergencies such as natural disasters. The embassy said it only held records of government scholarship students, so self-funded students should contact the embassy after arriving.</p>\n\n<h2>Is a Chinese degree recognised in Sri Lanka?</h2>\n<p>Degrees from recognised Chinese universities are used in Sri Lanka, but recognition is checked case by case. For jobs, further study or government posts, the University Grants Commission (UGC) can issue a certificate of recognition for a foreign qualification, and the Ministry of Foreign Affairs attests foreign degree certificates for official use.</p>\n<p>The Ministry of Foreign Affairs asks for the original degree certificate with copies certified either by the Sri Lankan embassy in the country that issued the degree or by that country''s embassy in Sri Lanka. For medicine the rule is stricter: the SLMC states that only graduates of SLMC-recognised institutions may register and practise in Sri Lanka, and foreign graduates must pass the ERPM. Check recognition before you enrol, not after you graduate.</p>\n\n<h2>Is China good for international students?</h2>\n<p>China suits Sri Lankan students who want an affordable, well-equipped university, a realistic chance of a full scholarship and a degree in engineering, science, business or medicine. China suits less well if you are not ready to adjust to a new language, a different teaching style and a strict visa and registration system.</p>\n<p>Learning some Mandarin before you go makes daily life much easier, even on an English-taught programme. A short HSK course in Sri Lanka is a useful first step.</p>\n\n<h2>How Proton Education Services can help</h2>\n<p>Proton Education Services is a Sri Lankan education consultancy with offices in Sri Lanka and Qingdao, China. We help with university and programme selection, admission and scholarship applications, CSCA preparation courses (Mathematics, Physics, Chemistry and Professional Chinese), X1/X2 visa support, and accommodation and arrival in China. See <a href=\"/services/education\">our study in China service</a>, <a href=\"/register\">register for a CSCA course</a> or <a href=\"/contact\">contact Proton</a> to talk through your options.</p>\n\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://english.shanghai.gov.cn/en-FAQs-StudyinShanghai/20251218/134bb78943f640519e2185ff83051f5d.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shanghai Municipal People''s Government – China Scholastic Competency Assessment: FAQs</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://govt.chinadaily.com.cn/s/201803/16/WS5cd90c60498e079e68020317/chinese-government-scholarship-full-scholarship.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">China Scholarship Council (via China Daily) – Chinese Government Scholarship: full scholarship</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.adaderana.lk/news/111470/30-sri-lankan-students-receive-chinese-govt-scholarships\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ada Derana – 30 Sri Lankan students receive Chinese govt scholarships</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://international.join-tsinghua.edu.cn/Admission1/Fees.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tsinghua University – International undergraduate admission: Fees</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://admission.whu.edu.cn/FRESHMEN_RELATED/VISA.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wuhan University – Student visa and residence permit</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://slmc.gov.lk/en/education\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sri Lanka Medical Council – Education: recognition and ERPM</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.mfa.gov.lk/degree-certificates-awarded-by-foreign-universities\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ministry of Foreign Affairs Sri Lanka – Degree certificates awarded by foreign universities</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://newsfirst.lk/2025/01/04/sri-lankan-embassy-in-china-requests-student-information-for-emergency-support\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Newsfirst – Sri Lankan Embassy in China requests student information for emergency support</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n</ul>","faq":[{"q":"Can I study in China after A/L in Sri Lanka?","a":"Yes. Sri Lankan students apply to Chinese bachelor''s programmes with their G.C.E. A/L results. Each university sets its own grade and subject expectations, so there is no national Z-score cut-off. Scholarship applicants for undergraduate places must also sit the CSCA, Chinese-taught programmes ask for an HSK certificate, and English-taught programmes usually need proof of English instead."},{"q":"Is studying in China cheaper than the UK or Australia?","a":"Usually, yes. Chinese universities publish modest international tuition fees; Tsinghua, one of the most expensive, lists CNY 26,000 to 40,000 a year for most undergraduate programmes. Living costs outside Beijing and Shanghai are lower still. A full Chinese Government Scholarship covers tuition, housing, insurance and a monthly stipend, which removes most of the cost."},{"q":"Do I need to know Chinese to study in China?","a":"No, not for English-taught programmes, which are common in engineering, business, IT and MBBS. You will still want some everyday Mandarin for shopping, transport and hospital visits. Chinese-taught degrees usually require HSK 4 or higher, and many students take a one-year Chinese language or foundation course before starting their degree."},{"q":"Is the CSCA compulsory for Sri Lankan students?","a":"The CSCA is mandatory for undergraduate applicants at Chinese Government Scholarship universities for 2026 and 2027. Some universities may also ask self-funded applicants for it, and a good score helps any application. Mathematics is required for everyone, and English-taught applicants are exempt only from Professional Chinese. Master''s and PhD applicants do not take it."},{"q":"Are Chinese medical degrees accepted in Sri Lanka?","a":"Only if the university is recognised by the Sri Lanka Medical Council. The SLMC says only graduates of recognised institutions may register to practise, and graduates of foreign medical schools must pass the Examination for Registration to Practise Medicine (ERPM). Check the SLMC''s recognised list for your exact university and programme before paying any fees."},{"q":"How long does the whole process take?","a":"Allow about nine to twelve months for a September intake. That covers choosing a programme, sitting the CSCA, HSK or IELTS, preparing documents, applying, waiting for the admission letter and JW form, and getting an X1 visa. Scholarship routes need the longest lead time because their deadlines fall early in the year."}]}')), SortOrder = 1, UpdatedDate = GETDATE()
WHERE Slug = 'study-in-china-from-sri-lanka'
GO

UPDATE syst.Guide SET ContentJSON = JSON_MODIFY(ContentJSON, '$.en', JSON_QUERY(N'{"title":"China Scholarship for Sri Lankan Students 2027: CSC Guide","seoTitle":"China Scholarship for Sri Lankan Students 2027 | Proton","description":"China scholarship for Sri Lankan students: what CSC pays, Type A vs Type B, eligibility, CSCA rule, 2027 deadlines and documents. Plan your application.","bodyHtml":"<p><strong>In short:</strong> the main China scholarship for Sri Lankan students is the Chinese Government Scholarship, run by the China Scholarship Council (CSC). A full CSC award pays tuition, a dormitory place, a monthly stipend of CNY 2,500–3,500 and medical insurance. Sri Lankans can apply through the Sri Lanka Ministry of Education (Type A) or directly through a Chinese university (Type B), and undergraduate applicants now also need a CSCA score.</p>\n<p>This guide covers what the CSC scholarship pays, the two application routes, eligibility, the 2027 timeline, documents and practical tips. For the bigger picture on studying in China, start with our <a href=\"/guides/study-in-china-from-sri-lanka\">complete guide to studying in China from Sri Lanka</a>.</p>\n<h2>What does the Chinese Government Scholarship cover?</h2>\n<p>A full Chinese Government Scholarship covers four things: a tuition waiver, free university accommodation (or a housing subsidy if the student lives off campus), a monthly living stipend paid by the host university, and comprehensive medical insurance. Partial awards cover only some of these items.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Monthly stipend (CNY)</th><th>Approx. in LKR</th><th>Off-campus housing subsidy (CNY/month)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Undergraduate</td><td>2,500</td><td>≈ 124,000</td><td>700</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Master''s / general scholar</td><td>3,000</td><td>≈ 149,000</td><td>700</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Doctoral / senior scholar</td><td>3,500</td><td>≈ 174,000</td><td>1,000</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Figures are the CSC rates as published in the official Chinese Government Scholarship description; confirm the current amounts on campuschina.org before you plan around them. LKR values use CNY 1 ≈ LKR 49.7 (indicative mid-market rate, CurrencyConvert.online, 23 Oct 2026). Most universities give scholarship students a shared twin room on campus; the housing subsidy applies only to students living off campus. Medical insurance is provided under China''s comprehensive insurance scheme for foreigners staying in China. Students who register on or before the 15th of a month receive that month''s full stipend; after the 15th, a half stipend.</p>\n<h2>Type A vs Type B: which CSC route should a Sri Lankan student use?</h2>\n<p>Sri Lankans can reach the Chinese Government Scholarship by two routes. Type A (bilateral) applications are nominated by the Sri Lanka Ministry of Education under a fixed country quota. Type B (Chinese University Program) applications go directly to a Chinese university that has its own CSC allocation. Both use the same online form, so choose the route that best fits your level and field.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th></th><th>Type A – bilateral</th><th>Type B – university program</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Who nominates you</td><td>Sri Lanka Ministry of Education (with the Chinese Embassy)</td><td>The Chinese university</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Agency number on CSC portal</td><td>The Sri Lanka number given in the MOE notice</td><td>The university''s own agency number (from its admissions page)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Number of places</td><td>Small national quota (see below)</td><td>Set by each university; no Sri Lanka-specific cap</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Typical levels</td><td>Mostly Master''s and PhD; occasional undergraduate quotas (e.g. Medicine)</td><td>Mainly Master''s and PhD; some universities offer undergraduate places</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Deadline</td><td>Set by the MOE notice, often earlier than China''s</td><td>Set by the university (confirm on its page)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Extra step</td><td>MOE shortlisting, sometimes an interview in Sri Lanka</td><td>A supervisor''s acceptance letter helps for research degrees</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<h3>How large is the Type A quota for Sri Lanka?</h3>\n<p>The Type A quota for Sri Lanka is small. Past Ministry of Education notices invited applications for 26 postgraduate (PhD and Master''s) scholarships for 2022/23, 25 postgraduate scholarships for 2024/25, and a separate call for five undergraduate Medicine scholarships for 2024/25 (applicants under 25 with Biological Science stream A/L passes). The Chinese Embassy presented 40 scholarships (7 doctoral, 24 Master''s, 9 undergraduate) in 2023 and 30 for the 2025–2026 academic year, and China has offered scholarships to Sri Lankans since 1975. Watch the <a href=\"https://moe.gov.lk/category/special-notices/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ministry of Education special notices</a> for the 2027/28 call; numbers change every year.</p>\n<h2>Who is eligible for the CSC scholarship?</h2>\n<p>To be eligible for the Chinese Government Scholarship, a Sri Lankan applicant must be a non-Chinese citizen in good physical and mental health, hold the required previous qualification, and be under the age limit for the level applied for. Type A calls from the Ministry of Education also require Sri Lankan citizenship.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Minimum qualification</th><th>Age limit (when applying)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Undergraduate</td><td>Completed secondary school (G.C.E. A/L)</td><td>Under 25</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Master''s</td><td>Bachelor''s degree</td><td>Under 35</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Doctoral</td><td>Master''s degree</td><td>Under 40</td></tr>\n<tr><td>General scholar</td><td>At least secondary school</td><td>Under 45</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Senior scholar</td><td>Master''s degree or associate professor</td><td>Under 50</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Language requirements depend on the teaching language. Chinese-taught undergraduate programmes ask for HSK 3 and Chinese-taught Master''s or PhD programmes ask for HSK 4; English-taught programmes ask for the university''s chosen proof, such as IELTS or TOEFL. See our <a href=\"/guides/learn-chinese-hsk-sri-lanka\">HSK guide for Sri Lankans</a> if you plan to study in Chinese.</p>\n<h2>Do CSC undergraduate applicants need the CSCA exam?</h2>\n<p>Yes. For the 2026 and 2027 academic years, every applicant for an undergraduate place at a Chinese Government Scholarship university must sit the China Scholastic Competency Assessment (CSCA) and upload a valid score report. Master''s and PhD applicants do not need the CSCA.</p>\n<p>The CSCA tests Mathematics (required for all programmes), Physics and/or Chemistry (at least one for science, engineering, agriculture and medicine), and Professional Chinese, which applicants to fully English-taught programmes are exempt from. The fee is CNY 450 for one subject or CNY 700 for two or more, and from 2026 the test runs about five times a year. For the September 2026 intake the qualifying sittings were 21 December 2025 and 25 January 2026, so expect the December 2026 and January 2027 sittings to matter for September 2027 – confirm on the official CSCA site. Read our <a href=\"/guides/csca-exam-sri-lanka\">CSCA exam guide</a> and <a href=\"/guides/csca-exam-registration\">how to register for the CSCA</a>.</p>\n<h2>What is the CSC scholarship 2027 deadline?</h2>\n<p>The CSC scholarship deadline for the September 2027 intake has not been published yet. In the 2026 cycle, Chinese embassies set Type A online deadlines in February 2026 (for example 8 and 22 February), so expect a similar window in early 2027. Type B deadlines are set by each university. Always use the date in your Ministry of Education notice or university page, not this estimate.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>When (typical – confirm)</th><th>What happens</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Oct–Dec 2026</td><td>Choose programmes; book and sit the CSCA (undergraduates); request recommendation letters</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Dec 2026–Jan 2027</td><td>CSC portal opens; MOE Type A notice expected; Type B university calls open</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Feb 2027</td><td>Likely Type A online deadline (based on February 2026)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Early–mid 2027</td><td>Type B university deadlines</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Jul–Aug 2027</td><td>Results published on the CSC portal; admission letter and JW201 visa form sent</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Sep 2027</td><td>Arrival and registration in China</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>For other intake dates see <a href=\"/guides/china-university-intakes-and-deadlines\">China university intakes and deadlines 2027</a>.</p>\n<h2>How to apply for the CSC scholarship (Type A or Type B)</h2>\n<p>Both routes use the same online form on campuschina.org; the difference is the programme category and agency number you enter. Follow these steps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Pick two or three programmes on campuschina.org and check each university accepts CSC students in your field and teaching language.</li>\n<li>Contact the university for a pre-admission document; for PhD and research Master''s, contact a supervisor first.</li>\n<li>Sit the CSCA if you are applying for an undergraduate programme.</li>\n<li>Register on the CSC portal, complete Personal Details, then select Program Category \"Type A\" (bilateral) or \"Type B\" (Chinese University Program).</li>\n<li>Enter the correct agency number: the Sri Lanka number in the MOE notice for Type A, or the university''s number for Type B. A mismatched number means the application is not processed.</li>\n<li>Complete the language and study plan section, upload all documents and submit. After submission, details cannot be edited unless you withdraw and resubmit before processing.</li>\n<li>For Type A, send the printed form and copies to the Ministry of Education as the notice instructs; for Type B, follow the university''s instructions.</li>\n<li>Track results on the portal; if successful, you receive an admission letter and JW201 form for your <a href=\"/guides/china-student-visa-sri-lanka\">China X1 student visa</a>.</li>\n</ol>\n<h2>What documents are required for a China scholarship?</h2>\n<p>The documents required for a China scholarship are the CSC application form, your passport information page, notarised copies of your highest qualification and transcripts, a study plan or research proposal, recommendation letters, language certificates, the Foreigner Physical Examination Form and a non-criminal record certificate.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Passport valid for at least 12 months beyond the study start date</li>\n<li>Notarised A/L certificate or degree certificate, with transcripts</li>\n<li>Study plan (at least 200 words for undergraduates) or research proposal (at least 800 words for postgraduates)</li>\n<li>Two recommendation letters from professors or associate professors (postgraduates)</li>\n<li>HSK, IELTS/TOEFL or other language proof; CSCA score report for undergraduates</li>\n<li>Pre-admission document from the university, if you have one</li>\n<li>Foreigner Physical Examination Form (valid for six months)</li>\n<li>Non-criminal record certificate, usually issued within six months of applying</li>\n<li>Portfolio for art and design applicants; guardian documents for applicants under 18</li>\n</ul>\n<p>How to get each one attested in Sri Lanka, including the police report and medical form, is covered in our <a href=\"/guides/documents-for-studying-in-china-sri-lanka\">documents guide for studying in China</a>.</p>\n<h2>Tips for a strong CSC application</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Decide your route early.</strong> Pick Type A or Type B based on your level and field, and match the agency number exactly.</li>\n<li><strong>Make the study plan specific.</strong> Name the programme, the modules or lab that interest you, and how the degree fits work you want to do in Sri Lanka.</li>\n<li><strong>Secure a supervisor for research degrees.</strong> A professor''s acceptance letter strengthens a Type B application.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep documents consistent.</strong> Your name must match the passport spelling on every certificate, letter and form.</li>\n<li><strong>Do the medical and police report last.</strong> Both are only accepted for about six months, so time them close to the deadline.</li>\n<li><strong>Plan the CSCA early.</strong> A late sitting may not return a score before the deadline.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Other China scholarships for Sri Lankan students</h2>\n<p>If you miss the CSC scholarship, other China scholarships are open to Sri Lankans. Provincial and city government scholarships usually cover tuition and sometimes accommodation. Many leading universities offer their own full or partial tuition waivers for international students. International Chinese Language Teacher Scholarships (the former Confucius Institute scholarship) fund Chinese-language study. Compare these with options in other countries in our <a href=\"/guides/foreign-scholarships-for-sri-lankan-students\">foreign scholarships hub for Sri Lankan students</a>, and check what a self-funded place would cost in the <a href=\"/guides/cost-of-studying-in-china-for-sri-lankans\">cost of studying in China guide</a>.</p>\n<h2>How Proton Education Services can help</h2>\n<p>Proton Education Services, with offices in Sri Lanka and Qingdao, helps students choose universities, prepare CSC and university scholarship applications, and get ready for the CSCA through online Mathematics, Physics, Chemistry and Chinese courses. See our <a href=\"/services/education\">study-in-China services</a> or <a href=\"/contact\">contact us</a> to talk through your options.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://govt.chinadaily.com.cn/s/201803/16/WS5cd90c60498e079e68020317/chinese-government-scholarship-full-scholarship.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">China Daily (Chinese Government Services) – Chinese Government Scholarship: Full Scholarship</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://montreal.china-consulate.gov.cn/jy/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Consulate General of China in Montreal – Competition for the 2026-2027 Chinese Government Scholarship</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://medan.china-consulate.gov.cn/indo/lsfw_16/zytz/202510/t20251027_11741719.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Consulate General of China in Medan – 2026/2027 Chinese Government Scholarship Application</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://english.shanghai.gov.cn/en-FAQs-StudyinShanghai/20251218/134bb78943f640519e2185ff83051f5d.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shanghai Municipal Government – China Scholastic Competency Assessment: FAQs</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://moe.gov.lk/category/special-notices/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sri Lanka Ministry of Education – Special notices (Chinese Government Scholarships Programme 2022/23 and 2024/25)</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.adaderana.lk/news/111470/30-sri-lankan-students-receive-chinese-govt-scholarships\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ada Derana – 30 Sri Lankan students receive Chinese govt scholarships (Aug 2025)</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://adaderana.lk/news/92471\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ada Derana – Chinese Embassy scholarship awards (Aug 2023)</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://studyat.nwpu.edu.cn/SCHOLARSHIPS1/Chinese_Government_Scholarships.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Northwestern Polytechnical University – Chinese Government Scholarships</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://currencyconvert.online/cny/lkr\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CurrencyConvert.online – CNY to LKR rate</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n</ul>","faq":[{"q":"Can Sri Lankan students get a full scholarship in China?","a":"Yes. The Chinese Government Scholarship (CSC) is a full award open to Sri Lankans that waives tuition, provides a dormitory place, pays a monthly stipend of CNY 2,500 to 3,500 depending on level, and includes medical insurance. Sri Lankans apply either through the Ministry of Education (Type A) or directly to a Chinese university with its own CSC places (Type B)."},{"q":"Is IELTS required for the Chinese Government Scholarship?","a":"Not always. The CSC itself does not demand IELTS, but each university sets the language proof for its English-taught programmes, and IELTS or TOEFL are common choices. Chinese-taught programmes ask for HSK 3 at undergraduate level or HSK 4 for Master''s and PhD instead. Check the specific programme page before booking a test."},{"q":"How many CSC scholarships does Sri Lanka get each year?","a":"The Type A bilateral quota is small and changes every year. Recent Ministry of Education calls offered around 25 to 26 postgraduate places, sometimes with a separate undergraduate Medicine call, and the Chinese Embassy presented 30 scholarships for 2025-2026. Type B places offered through individual universities are additional and are not capped by country."},{"q":"Do I need the CSCA for a Master''s or PhD CSC scholarship?","a":"No. The CSCA requirement applies to undergraduate applicants at Chinese Government Scholarship universities for the 2026 and 2027 academic years. Master''s and PhD applicants are assessed on their degree results, research proposal, recommendation letters and language proof instead, so they can skip the CSCA."},{"q":"When should I start preparing for the 2027 CSC scholarship?","a":"Start in October to December 2026. That gives time to sit the CSCA if you are an undergraduate, contact universities or supervisors, request recommendation letters and notarise certificates. Deadlines in the 2026 cycle fell in February, so leaving preparation until the portal opens usually means a rushed, weaker application."}]}')), SortOrder = 2, UpdatedDate = GETDATE()
WHERE Slug = 'chinese-government-scholarship-sri-lanka'
