IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM syst.Guide WHERE Slug = 'documents-for-studying-in-china-sri-lanka')
    INSERT INTO syst.Guide (Slug, CoverImageURL, Icon, Color, ContentJSON, SortOrder, IsActive)
    VALUES ('documents-for-studying-in-china-sri-lanka', '', 'file-text', 'slate', N'{"en":{"title":"Documents Required to Study in China from Sri Lanka: Full Checklist","seoTitle":"Documents Required to Study in China from Sri Lanka | Proton","description":"Documents required to study in China from Sri Lanka: certificates, notarisation, MFA attestation, police report and medical form. Get the checklist.","bodyHtml":"<p><strong>In short:</strong> the documents required to study in China from Sri Lanka are a valid passport, your A/L (and O/L) certificates, notarised copies or English translations, a police clearance certificate, the Foreigner Physical Examination Form, proof of funds, a study plan, recommendation letters and photos. For the visa you add the university admission letter and the JW201/JW202 form. Start collecting them at least three to four months before your application deadline, because attestation and the police report take the longest.</p>\n<p>This guide lists every document by stage (university admission, CSC scholarship and visa), who issues each one in Sri Lanka, what it costs where a fee is published, and the order to get them in. For the full picture of studying in China, start with our <a href=\"/guides/study-in-china-from-sri-lanka\">complete guide to studying in China from Sri Lanka</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>What documents are required to study in China from Sri Lanka?</h2>\n<p>The documents required to study in China from Sri Lanka fall into three stages. A Chinese university needs your academic records and identity documents to admit you; a CSC scholarship application adds a medical form, a police report and stronger supporting letters; and the visa needs the admission letter and JW form. Most documents are reused across all three stages.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Who issues it (Sri Lanka)</th><th>Needed for</th><th>Approx. cost</th><th>Processing time</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Passport</td><td>Department of Immigration and Emigration</td><td>Admission, CSC, visa</td><td>Check current fee</td><td>Check</td></tr>\n<tr><td>A/L and O/L certificates</td><td>Department of Examinations (certificate.doenets.lk or One-Day Service)</td><td>Admission, CSC</td><td>Check on doenets.lk</td><td>Check</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Degree certificate and transcript (postgraduates)</td><td>Your university''s examinations or registrar''s office</td><td>Admission, CSC</td><td>Set by the university</td><td>Set by the university</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Notarised copies and translations</td><td>Sri Lankan notary public (attorney-at-law); sworn translator for Sinhala or Tamil documents</td><td>Admission, CSC</td><td>Check with the notary</td><td>Check</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Foreign Ministry attestation</td><td>Consular Affairs Division, Ministry of Foreign Affairs (e-DAS)</td><td>Only if the university or a Chinese authority asks</td><td>Rs. 800 per exam certificate, Rs. 1,200 for other documents (2023 gazette rates; check)</td><td>Check</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Police clearance certificate</td><td>Sri Lanka Police (police.lk E-Services)</td><td>CSC, many universities</td><td>Rs. 5,100 listed for applicants abroad (check the in-country rate on the portal)</td><td>About 14 days after payment</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Foreigner Physical Examination Form</td><td>A public hospital in Sri Lanka</td><td>CSC, some universities, residence permit in China</td><td>Check with the hospital</td><td>Check</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bank statement or sponsor letter</td><td>Your or your sponsor''s bank</td><td>Admission, visa (self-funded)</td><td>Bank charge</td><td>Check with your bank</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Study plan and recommendation letters</td><td>You; your teachers or lecturers</td><td>Admission, CSC</td><td>Free</td><td>Allow 2 to 4 weeks for referees</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Admission letter and JW201/JW202</td><td>The Chinese university</td><td>Visa</td><td>Free</td><td>Sent after admission</td></tr>\n<tr><td>X1 student visa</td><td>Chinese Embassy, Colombo</td><td>Travel</td><td>LKR 5,850 single entry (valid to 31 Dec 2026)</td><td>About 4 working days</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>\"Check\" means no current official figure could be confirmed for this guide in October 2026; confirm with the issuing office before you pay.</p>\n\n<h2>How do I get my A/L and O/L certificates for a Chinese university?</h2>\n<p>Sri Lankan A/L and O/L certificates are issued by the Department of Examinations. You can apply online on the Department''s certificate portal (certificate.doenets.lk), which produces a digital certificate, or use the Department''s One-Day Service if a university wants a printed original. Chinese universities usually ask for a scan at application and the original or a notarised copy on arrival.</p>\n<p>When applying online, the certificate type matters. If you also need Foreign Ministry attestation, choose \"Foreign Ministry Certificate (Direct to Foreign Ministry)\": the certificate goes straight to the Consular Affairs Division and you receive an SMS with a certificate number to start authentication. The Ministry says this online route covers certificates issued from 2001 onwards, and fees are paid separately to the Department of Examinations and to the Ministry.</p>\n<p>Degree holders applying for a Master''s or PhD also need their degree certificate and full transcript in English from their university.</p>\n\n<h2>Do I need notarisation and translation of documents in Sri Lanka?</h2>\n<p>Notarisation of documents in Sri Lanka is the most common requirement. The CSC and most Chinese universities ask for notarised copies of your highest qualification and transcripts, which means a Sri Lankan notary public (a practising attorney-at-law) certifies that each copy matches the original. Documents in Sinhala or Tamil, such as a birth certificate, need an English translation by a sworn translator first.</p>\n<p>Exam certificates from the Department of Examinations are already in English, so they usually need notarisation only. Notary fees are set by each notary; ask for a per-page price, and notarise a few spare sets because universities often keep copies.</p>\n\n<h2>Is Foreign Ministry attestation needed, and how does MOFA attestation online work?</h2>\n<p>Foreign Ministry attestation in Sri Lanka is done by the Consular Affairs Division of the Ministry of Foreign Affairs through its Electronic Documents Attestation System (e-DAS). Not every Chinese university asks for it, so check your university''s instructions first. Where it is required, documents issued by Sri Lankan institutions must be attested through e-DAS.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>O/L and A/L certificates:</strong> MOFA attestation online is fully available. Apply on the Department of Examinations portal with the Foreign Ministry certificate type, then follow the SMS link to the Ministry''s consular portal and pay the attestation fee online.</li>\n<li><strong>Birth certificates:</strong> online authentication has been available since September 2024, with the authenticated certificate sent by email.</li>\n<li><strong>Degree certificates and other documents:</strong> degree certificate attestation in Sri Lanka goes through e-DAS after the issuing institution has entered the document, and may need a visit to the Consular Affairs Division (16th Floor, Suhurupaya, Battaramulla). Confirm the current appointment system and visiting hours on mfa.gov.lk before going.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The fees gazetted from 1 January 2023 were Rs. 800 per examination certificate and Rs. 1,200 for other documents. These were the latest published rates found in October 2026; confirm them on mfa.gov.lk before paying.</p>\n\n<h2>Does Sri Lanka use the Apostille, or do I need Chinese Embassy legalisation?</h2>\n<p>China has applied the Hague Apostille Convention since 7 November 2023, but Sri Lanka is not a party: it does not appear on the Hague Conference status table as of October 2026. A Sri Lankan document therefore cannot be apostilled for use in China. If a Chinese authority wants a fully legalised document, the route is Foreign Ministry attestation in Sri Lanka followed by consular legalisation at the Chinese Embassy in Colombo.</p>\n<p>In practice, most Chinese universities and the CSC accept notarised copies for admission, and full Embassy legalisation is needed only when a specific university, province or later process asks for it. We could not find a published Chinese Embassy legalisation fee schedule, so contact its consular section (+94 11 2688610, chinaemb_lk@mfa.gov.cn) for current requirements and fees before you go.</p>\n\n<h2>How do I apply for a police clearance certificate in Sri Lanka online?</h2>\n<p>A police clearance certificate in Sri Lanka is applied for online on the Sri Lanka Police website: go to police.lk, open E-Services and choose Clearance Certificates. You fill in the form, upload scans of your passport and NIC, and pay by card. Police notices from 2026 say the certificate is issued in about 14 days after payment is confirmed.</p>\n<p>The fee listed for Sri Lankans applying from abroad is Rs. 5,100 (Sri Lanka Police notice, March 2026); check the amount shown on the portal when you apply from Sri Lanka. Give accurate, complete details, because errors delay the police report. For the CSC and most Chinese universities, a police report for visa and admission purposes should be issued within about six months of your application, so do not apply too early.</p>\n\n<h2>What is the Foreigner Physical Examination Form for China?</h2>\n<p>The Foreigner Physical Examination Form is China''s standard medical form for international students. A doctor completes it after a physical check, chest X-ray, ECG and blood tests that include at least HIV and syphilis. The form is valid for six months from the examination date, and it must still be valid when you arrive in China, or you may have to repeat the tests there.</p>\n<p>Download the form from your university or the CSC portal and have it done at a public hospital in Colombo or your district. Chinese guidance says reports from private hospitals may need to be notarised, so a government hospital is the simpler choice. The doctor must sign and stamp the form, your photo should carry the hospital stamp, and the original X-ray, ECG and lab reports must be attached. Fees vary by hospital; ask before booking.</p>\n\n<h2>What else do I need: bank statement, passport, study plan, photos?</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Passport:</strong> the Chinese Embassy requires more than six months'' validity and a blank visa page; the CSC asks for at least 12 months beyond your start date. A passport valid for the whole course avoids a renewal in China.</li>\n<li><strong>Bank statement or financial guarantee:</strong> self-funded students need bank statements showing at least USD 2,500 for each year of study for the visa. A parent''s account works with a sponsorship letter and proof of relationship.</li>\n<li><strong>Study plan:</strong> at least 200 words for undergraduates and an 800-word research proposal for postgraduates under CSC rules.</li>\n<li><strong>Recommendation letters:</strong> two signed letters on letterhead. Postgraduates need professors or associate professors; undergraduates usually ask their principal or A/L teachers.</li>\n<li><strong>Language proof:</strong> HSK for Chinese-taught programmes, IELTS or TOEFL for English-taught ones; see our <a href=\"/guides/learn-chinese-hsk-sri-lanka\">HSK guide</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Photos:</strong> a recent colour photo, 48 x 33 mm, white background, for the visa form; keep digital copies for applications.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The full list of documents required for the China scholarship (CSC), including the CSCA score for undergraduates, is in our <a href=\"/guides/chinese-government-scholarship-sri-lanka\">Chinese Government Scholarship guide</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>In what order should I prepare the documents?</h2>\n<p>Prepare documents that do not expire first and time-limited documents last. Certificates and notarised copies can be done months ahead; the police report and medical form are accepted for only about six months, so get them close to your scholarship or admission deadline. Check dates in our <a href=\"/guides/china-university-intakes-and-deadlines\">intakes and deadlines guide</a>.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>4 to 6 months before the deadline:</strong> check passport validity and renew if needed. Order A/L, O/L and degree certificates and transcripts.</li>\n<li><strong>3 to 4 months before:</strong> get notarised copies and any translations. Start Foreign Ministry attestation if your university requires it.</li>\n<li><strong>2 to 3 months before:</strong> write your study plan and ask referees for letters. Book HSK, IELTS or the CSCA if needed.</li>\n<li><strong>6 to 8 weeks before:</strong> apply for the police clearance certificate (about 14 days) and complete the Foreigner Physical Examination Form.</li>\n<li><strong>2 to 4 weeks before:</strong> get a fresh bank statement and scan everything as clear PDFs named by document.</li>\n<li><strong>After admission:</strong> receive the admission letter and JW201/JW202, then apply for the X1 visa online and submit at the Chinese Embassy in Colombo; see our <a href=\"/guides/china-student-visa-sri-lanka\">China student visa guide</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Before flying:</strong> carry originals, notarised copies, the medical form with original reports, and spare photos.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>How Proton Education Services can help</h2>\n<p>Proton Education Services checks your document set against your chosen university''s and the CSC''s requirements, so nothing is missing or expired at submission. The team also handles admission applications and X1/X2 visa support from offices in Sri Lanka and Qingdao. See our <a href=\"/services/education\">study-in-China service</a> or <a href=\"/contact\">contact Proton</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.hcch.net/en/instruments/conventions/status-table/?cid=41\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HCCH – Apostille Convention status table</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.adaderana.lk/news/119429/notice-on-online-process-to-obtain-police-clearance-certificates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ada Derana – Notice on online process to obtain police clearance certificates</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://abudhabi.embassy.gov.lk/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/Notice-Police-Clearance-Certificate-Online.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Embassy of Sri Lanka, Abu Dhabi – Police Clearance Certificate online notice</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://mfa.gov.lk/?p=71704\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sri Lanka Ministry of Foreign Affairs – Online authentication of GCE O/L and A/L certificates</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.mfa.gov.lk/certificates-issued-by-the-department-of-examinations\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sri Lanka Ministry of Foreign Affairs – Certificates issued by the Department of Examinations</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://mfa.gov.lk/en/?p=39734\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sri Lanka Ministry of Foreign Affairs – Consular Affairs Division and e-DAS</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://english.newsfirst.lk/2024/9/5/e-authentication-for-vital-certificates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Newsfirst – E-authentication for vital certificates</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.adaderana.lk/news/87224/revised-consular-fees-for-attestation-services-effective-from-jan-01\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ada Derana – Revised consular fees for attestation services from 1 Jan 2023</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://english.beijing.gov.cn/studyinginbeijing/youmayneed/tips/202409/t20240903_3788643.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Beijing Municipal Government – A Guide to Health Entrance Examination</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://lk.china-embassy.gov.cn/eng/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Embassy of the People''s Republic of China in Sri Lanka – visa and consular information</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n</ul>","faq":[{"q":"Does Sri Lanka issue apostilles for documents used in China?","a":"No. China has applied the Apostille Convention since 7 November 2023, but Sri Lanka is not a party, so Sri Lankan documents cannot be apostilled. Where full legalisation is required, documents are attested by the Foreign Ministry''s Consular Affairs Division and then legalised by the Chinese Embassy in Colombo. Many universities accept notarised copies instead, so check first."},{"q":"How long is a Sri Lankan police report valid for a China scholarship?","a":"Chinese universities and the CSC generally expect a non-criminal record certificate issued within about six months of your application. Apply through the police.lk E-Services portal roughly six to eight weeks before your deadline, since police notices say processing takes around 14 days after payment is confirmed."},{"q":"Can I get my A/L certificate attested online?","a":"Yes, for certificates issued from 2001 onwards. Apply on the Department of Examinations certificate portal, choose the Foreign Ministry certificate type, and complete authentication through the link the Ministry sends by SMS. Fees are paid online separately to the Department of Examinations and to the Consular Affairs Division."},{"q":"Where can I do the Foreigner Physical Examination Form in Sri Lanka?","a":"Use a government hospital, because Chinese guidance says reports from private hospitals may need extra notarisation. Bring the official form, a photo and your passport. The doctor signs and stamps the form and attaches the X-ray, ECG and blood test reports. The form stays valid for six months, and must still be valid when you arrive in China."},{"q":"How much money must I show for a China student visa?","a":"The Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka asks self-funded students for bank statements showing at least USD 2,500 for each year of study. Scholarship students submit proof of the scholarship instead. A parent''s account is acceptable with a sponsorship letter and proof of relationship such as a birth certificate."}]}}', 7, 'A')
GO

UPDATE syst.Guide SET ContentJSON = JSON_MODIFY(ContentJSON, '$.en', JSON_QUERY(N'{"title":"China Student Visa for Sri Lankans (X1 and X2): Requirements, Fees and Steps","seoTitle":"China Student Visa Sri Lanka: X1/X2 Guide | Proton","description":"China student visa Sri Lanka guide: X1 vs X2, documents checklist, JW202, Colombo embassy fees and steps, and the 30-day residence permit. Read first.","bodyHtml":"<p><strong>In short:</strong> a China student visa for Sri Lankans is an X1 visa (study longer than 180 days) or an X2 visa (180 days or less). Sri Lankan students apply online through the China Visa Online Application System, then hand in their passport and give fingerprints in person at the Chinese Embassy in Colombo. For a Sri Lankan passport, the single-entry visa fee is LKR 5,850 under the Embassy''s reduced-fee notice valid to 31 December 2026, and normal processing takes about 4 working days after submission.</p><p>This guide covers the China student visa process from Sri Lanka step by step: which visa you need, the documents checklist, fees, the JW201/JW202 form, and what you must do in the first 30 days after landing in China. For the wider picture, start with our <a href=\"/guides/study-in-china-from-sri-lanka\">complete guide to studying in China from Sri Lanka</a>.</p><h2>X1 or X2: which China student visa do Sri Lankans need?</h2><p>The China student visa type depends only on how long your programme lasts. Degree students (bachelor''s, master''s, PhD, MBBS) and anyone on a language or foundation course longer than 180 days need an X1 visa. Short courses, exchanges and summer schools of 180 days or less use an X2 visa.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Point</th><th>X1 visa</th><th>X2 visa</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Study length</td><td>More than 180 days</td><td>180 days or less</td></tr><tr><td>Typical use</td><td>Bachelor''s, master''s, PhD, MBBS, one-year Chinese language programmes</td><td>Short courses, exchange semesters, summer and winter schools</td></tr><tr><td>Stay allowed on the visa</td><td>30 days from entry; you then convert it to a residence permit</td><td>The duration printed on the visa</td></tr><tr><td>Physical examination record</td><td>Required (for the residence permit)</td><td>Only if staying over six months</td></tr><tr><td>Residence permit</td><td>Apply within 30 days of entry</td><td>Not required</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka states that X2 visa applicants follow the same document requirements as X1 applicants.</p><h2>China student visa requirements: documents checklist</h2><p>The China student visa requirements for Sri Lankan applicants are set by the Chinese Embassy in Colombo. The core X1 visa documents are your passport, a photo, the online application form, the university admission letter, the JW201 or JW202 form, and proof of funding.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Document</th><th>Details from the Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Passport</td><td>Original, valid for more than 6 months, with at least one blank visa page, plus a copy of the photo page. Bring old passports if you have them.</td></tr><tr><td>Photo</td><td>Recent colour photo, 48 x 33 mm, white background, facing forward, bareheaded. Uploaded with the online form.</td></tr><tr><td>Online application form</td><td>Completed and submitted on the China Visa Online Application System; print and sign the confirmation page.</td></tr><tr><td>Admission letter</td><td>Original and photocopy, issued by the Chinese university.</td></tr><tr><td>JW201 or JW202 form</td><td>Original and photocopy of the \"Visa Application for Study in China\" form.</td></tr><tr><td>Proof of funding</td><td>Scholarship students: proof of the scholarship. Self-funded students: bank statements showing at least USD 2,500 for each year of study.</td></tr><tr><td>Proof of legal stay</td><td>Only if you are not a Sri Lankan citizen applying in Sri Lanka.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The Embassy can ask for extra documents or an interview, and its X visa page says X1 and X2 applicants must attend an interview. For attested certificates, police reports and the medical form itself, see our <a href=\"/guides/documents-for-studying-in-china-sri-lanka\">documents guide for studying in China</a>.</p><h2>JW201 vs JW202: what is the difference?</h2><p>The JW201 and JW202 are both the \"Visa Application for Study in China\" form, issued by your Chinese university together with the admission letter. You do not fill in or buy either form yourself; the university sends it with your offer pack.</p><ul><li><strong>JW201</strong> is issued to students on Chinese Government (CSC) and other government-funded scholarships.</li><li><strong>JW202</strong> is issued to self-funded students and those on university or other non-government scholarships.</li></ul><p>For the visa, the JW201 and JW202 forms work the same way. Check that the name, date of birth and passport number on the JW form match your passport exactly, because a mismatch has to be corrected by the university. If you are applying for a CSC award, see our <a href=\"/guides/chinese-government-scholarship-sri-lanka\">Chinese Government Scholarship guide</a>.</p><h2>How to apply for a China student visa in Colombo, step by step</h2><p>Since 30 June 2025, the Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka takes visa applications through the China Visa Online Application System, followed by an in-person passport submission at the Embassy in Colombo. The Embassy''s 2025 guide says it does not accept postal applications or online payment.</p><ol><li><strong>Get your admission letter and JW201/JW202</strong> from the university. Universities usually send these a few weeks before the intake; see our <a href=\"/guides/china-university-intakes-and-deadlines\">China university intakes and deadlines</a> to plan backwards.</li><li><strong>Apply online</strong> at <a href=\"https://consular.mfa.gov.cn/VISA/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">consular.mfa.gov.cn/VISA</a>: create an account with your email, fill in the form, upload your photo and documents, and click \"Confirm and Submit\". The Embassy does a preliminary review, usually in 1 to 3 working days.</li><li><strong>Visit the Embassy</strong> once the system shows \"Please visit the Embassy/Consulate\". No separate appointment is needed. Submission hours are 8:30 am to 11:30 am on working days. Bring your original passport, the printed and signed form and original documents; fingerprints are taken unless you are exempt.</li><li><strong>Pay the fee</strong> at the Embassy using the pick-up slip, between 2:00 pm and 5:00 pm. The Embassy fee page lists card and mobile payment options and says cash is not accepted; confirm on the day.</li><li><strong>Collect your passport</strong> with the visa. Normal processing is about 4 working days (the 2025 guide says 3 to 4 working days after submission).</li></ol><p>The Embassy recommends applying 2 to 3 weeks before you travel. Embassy visa contact: 0094-112676658 or colombo@csm.mfa.gov.cn.</p><h2>China student visa cost for Sri Lankans</h2><p>The China student visa fee for a Sri Lankan passport holder is LKR 5,850 for a single-entry visa, under a visa fee reduction that the Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka extended to 31 December 2026. X1 visas are normally single entry. Express and rush services cost extra.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item (Sri Lankan passport)</th><th>Fee (LKR)</th><th>Processing</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Single entry</td><td>5,850</td><td>Normal: about 4 working days</td></tr><tr><td>Double entry</td><td>8,955</td><td>Normal</td></tr><tr><td>Express service (extra)</td><td>+9,140</td><td>3 working days</td></tr><tr><td>Rush service (extra)</td><td>+13,440</td><td>2 working days</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Fees as listed on the Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka website, accessed 23 October 2026. Express fees are not refunded if processing is delayed. If you apply in 2027 for a September 2027 intake, check whether the reduced fees have been extended. The Embassy''s guide does not mention a separate visa centre service fee in Colombo. For tuition, housing and living costs, see our <a href=\"/guides/cost-of-studying-in-china-for-sri-lankans\">cost of studying in China for Sri Lankans</a>.</p><h2>Do you need a physical examination for an X1 visa?</h2><p>Yes, an X1 student needs a Foreigner Physical Examination record, but it is used for the residence permit in China rather than checked at the Embassy in Colombo. Universities usually include the form in the offer pack and ask you to bring it completed, with lab reports and a chest X-ray.</p><p>After arrival, the city''s International Travel Healthcare Center verifies your overseas record or asks you to repeat the tests. Shanghai Jiao Tong University, for example, quotes about RMB 500 for a full exam in China, or RMB 70 to 400 to verify an overseas record (about LKR 3,500 to 19,900 at CNY 1 &asymp; LKR 49.7, CurrencyConvert.online, 23 Oct 2026). Follow your own university''s instructions.</p><h2>After you arrive: residence permit and 24-hour registration</h2><p>An X1 visa is only an entry visa. Under China''s Exit and Entry Administration Law, a student holding an X1 visa must apply for a study residence permit within 30 days of entry, at the exit-entry office of the local public security bureau.</p><ul><li><strong>Within 24 hours:</strong> register your accommodation. Hotels do this for you. If you live in a private flat, you or your landlord must register at the local police station within 24 hours of arrival (Article 39). Universities usually handle registration for students in campus dormitories.</li><li><strong>Within 30 days:</strong> complete university registration, the health check verification and the residence permit application (Article 30). The university''s international office normally prepares the paperwork.</li></ul><p>Missing the 30-day residence permit deadline can mean fines for overstaying, so treat it as a firm date.</p><h2>Common reasons a China student visa is refused or delayed</h2><p>The Chinese Embassy can refuse a visa without giving reasons, and fees are not refunded. In practice, most problems come from paperwork that can be avoided:</p><ul><li>Passport with less than 6 months'' validity or no blank visa page.</li><li>Name or passport number on the JW201/JW202 not matching the passport.</li><li>Photo that fails the 48 x 33 mm, white-background rule.</li><li>Self-funded applicants without bank statements showing at least USD 2,500 per study year.</li><li>Applying on the wrong visa type, such as a tourist visa; universities enrol students only on X1 or X2 visas.</li><li>Inconsistent answers between the online form and the interview.</li></ul><h2>Can Sri Lankan students work part-time in China?</h2><p>Not freely. Under Article 43 of the Exit and Entry Administration Law, a foreign student who works in breach of the rules for foreign students is treated as working illegally. Off-campus internships need university approval and an internship annotation on the residence permit, added by the public security bureau.</p><p>Peking University, for example, caps each annotation at six months and requires a study residence permit and good academic standing. Rules differ by city and university, so do not plan your budget around part-time income.</p><h2>How Proton Education Services can help</h2><p>Proton Education Services, with offices in Sri Lanka and Qingdao, checks your JW form, documents and online visa form before you visit the Embassy, and helps with arrival, accommodation and the residence permit step in China. See our <a href=\"/services/education\">study in China services</a> or <a href=\"/contact\">contact Proton</a> on WhatsApp +94 74 201 6755.</p><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li><a href=\"http://lk.china-embassy.gov.cn/eng/lsfw/202506/t20250620_11653845.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka: Visa Application Guide Version 2025</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"http://lk.china-embassy.gov.cn/eng/lsfw/qz/201804/t20180416_1999850.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka: Visa requirements (X1/X2)</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"http://lk.china-embassy.gov.cn/eng/lsfw/201806/t20180602_1439977.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka: Consular fee</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"http://lk.china-embassy.gov.cn/eng/zytz/202501/t20250102_11526013.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka: Extension of visa fee reduction</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://consular.mfa.gov.cn/VISA/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ministry of Foreign Affairs of China: China Visa Online Application System</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://www.adaderana.lk/news.php?nid=109598\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ada Derana: Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka to launch new online visa application system</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://us.china-embassy.gov.cn/eng/unshown/visas/zyxx/201307/t20130702_4899883.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chinese Embassy in the USA: Exit and Entry Administration Law of the PRC</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://en.sjtu.edu.cn/study-sjtu/current/services/74\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shanghai Jiao Tong University: Visa and Residence Permit</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://isd.pku.edu.cn/en/campus_detail.php?id=410\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Peking University: Off-campus internship for international students</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li>CurrencyConvert.online: CNY to LKR rate, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li></ul>","faq":[{"q":"How long does a China student visa take in Sri Lanka?","a":"Plan on one to two weeks in total. The online preliminary review usually takes 1 to 3 working days, and the Embassy in Colombo then processes the passport in about 4 working days on the normal service. Express (3 working days) and rush (2 working days) cost extra. The Embassy suggests applying 2 to 3 weeks before travel."},{"q":"Is there a China Visa Application Service Center in Colombo?","a":"The Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka''s current guide tells applicants to apply online on the China Visa Online Application System and then submit the passport and fingerprints at the Embassy itself, from 8:30 am to 11:30 am on working days. Check the Embassy website before you go, as arrangements can change."},{"q":"Can I apply for a China student visa without the JW202 form?","a":"No. The Embassy lists the JW201 or JW202 form, original and copy, as a required document for X1 and X2 visas, together with the admission letter. Only your Chinese university can issue the form, so wait for the offer pack before starting the online visa application."},{"q":"Do I need a bank statement for a China student visa?","a":"Self-funded students do. The Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka asks for bank statements showing at least USD 2,500 for each year of study in China. Scholarship holders can submit proof of their scholarship instead, such as the CSC award letter, in place of personal bank statements."},{"q":"Can I enter China on a tourist visa and change to a student visa?","a":"Do not plan on it. Universities enrol international students only on X1 or X2 visas, and Shanghai Jiao Tong University, for example, says students who enter on any other visa cannot enrol. Get the correct X visa from the Embassy in Colombo before you fly."}]}')), SortOrder = 8, UpdatedDate = GETDATE()
WHERE Slug = 'china-student-visa-sri-lanka'
GO

UPDATE syst.Guide SET ContentJSON = JSON_MODIFY(ContentJSON, '$.en', JSON_QUERY(N'{"title":"Cost of Studying in China for Sri Lankan Students (2026-27)","seoTitle":"Cost of Studying in China for Sri Lankans | Proton","description":"Cost of studying in China for Sri Lankan students: real tuition, dorm, living and visa costs in CNY and LKR, plus a 4-year budget. See the full breakdown.","bodyHtml":"<p><strong>In short:</strong> the cost of studying in China for Sri Lankan students is mostly tuition plus living costs. On the official fee pages we checked, a bachelor''s degree costs about CNY 17,000 to 24,600 a year in tuition (roughly LKR 0.85 to 1.2 million), MBBS costs CNY 34,000 to 75,000 a year, and a student needs about CNY 1,200 to 2,500 a month to live. A full Chinese Government (CSC) scholarship removes tuition, dormitory and insurance and adds a monthly stipend, which cuts the total by more than 80 percent.</p>\n<p>All rupee figures in this guide use one rate: <strong>CNY 1 &asymp; LKR 49.7</strong> (indicative mid-market rate, CurrencyConvert.online, 23 Oct 2026), rounded. University fees below are as listed on each university''s own page when we checked in October 2026; some pages are not dated, so confirm the current figure with the university before you pay anything. For the full picture of studying in China, start with our <a href=\"/guides/study-in-china-from-sri-lanka\">complete guide to studying in China from Sri Lanka</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>How much does it cost to study in China per year?</h2>\n<p>A self-funded Sri Lankan student at a typical Chinese university should budget roughly CNY 40,000 to 55,000 a year (about LKR 2 to 2.7 million) for a bachelor''s degree, including tuition, a shared dormitory room, insurance and living costs. MBBS and big-city universities sit at the top of that range or above it.</p>\n<p>The four parts of the cost of studying in China are tuition, accommodation, compulsory fees (insurance and residence permit) and daily living. Tuition is the largest part and varies most by subject, level and university. Living costs vary mainly by city.</p>\n\n<h2>China university fees for international students by level</h2>\n<p>China university fees for international students are set by each university and depend on level, subject and teaching language. Medicine is the most expensive subject; Chinese-taught humanities programmes are the cheapest. The table shows examples from official university fee pages.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Example (university, city)</th><th>Tuition per year (CNY)</th><th>Approx. LKR per year</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Chinese language programme</td><td>China University of Petroleum, Qingdao</td><td>14,000 (7,000 per semester)</td><td>&asymp; 696,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bachelor''s</td><td>China University of Petroleum, Qingdao</td><td>17,000&ndash;20,000</td><td>&asymp; 845,000&ndash;994,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Bachelor''s</td><td>Tongji University, Shanghai</td><td>20,000 (liberal arts) to 24,600 (sciences)</td><td>&asymp; 994,000&ndash;1.22 million</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Master''s</td><td>China University of Petroleum, Qingdao</td><td>20,000&ndash;24,000</td><td>&asymp; 994,000&ndash;1.19 million</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Master''s</td><td>Tongji University, Shanghai</td><td>22,960 (liberal arts) to 28,700 (sciences)</td><td>&asymp; 1.14&ndash;1.43 million</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PhD</td><td>China University of Petroleum, Qingdao</td><td>26,000&ndash;30,000</td><td>&asymp; 1.29&ndash;1.49 million</td></tr>\n<tr><td>PhD</td><td>Tongji University, Shanghai</td><td>30,000 (liberal arts) to 41,000 (sciences)</td><td>&asymp; 1.49&ndash;2.04 million</td></tr>\n<tr><td>MBBS (English-taught)</td><td>Jiangsu University, Zhenjiang</td><td>34,000</td><td>&asymp; 1.69 million</td></tr>\n<tr><td>MBBS (English-taught)</td><td>Dalian Medical University, Dalian</td><td>42,000</td><td>&asymp; 2.09 million</td></tr>\n<tr><td>MBBS (English-taught)</td><td>Fudan University, Shanghai</td><td>75,000</td><td>&asymp; 3.73 million</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>China University of Petroleum, Tongji and Fudan are among the universities Proton Education Services works with. English-taught programmes usually cost more than Chinese-taught ones at the same university. MBA programmes are priced separately and can be much higher (Tongji lists its full-time English IMBA at CNY 89,000 a year). MBBS runs for six years, so read our <a href=\"/guides/mbbs-in-china-sri-lanka\">MBBS in China guide</a> for the full medical-degree budget and SLMC rules.</p>\n\n<h2>What is the China university accommodation fee?</h2>\n<p>A shared twin room in a Chinese university dormitory usually costs CNY 400 to 750 per person per month, or about LKR 20,000 to 37,000. Single rooms and off-campus flats cost noticeably more, especially in Shanghai and Beijing.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>China University of Petroleum (Qingdao):</strong> twin room CNY 500&ndash;750 per person per month, plus a CNY 1,000 room deposit. Off-campus rooms arranged through the university are CNY 1,100 (twin, per person) to 2,200 (single) per month.</li>\n<li><strong>Jiangsu University (Zhenjiang):</strong> double room CNY 4,900 per student per academic year.</li>\n<li><strong>Tongji University (Shanghai):</strong> international student buildings listed at about CNY 35&ndash;80 per room per day, depending on the building.</li>\n<li><strong>Shanghai Jiao Tong University:</strong> estimates an off-campus two-room flat at CNY 2,000&ndash;4,000 a month near the Minhang campus and CNY 3,500&ndash;4,500 near Xuhui.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Electricity, heating and deposits are charged differently at each university, so ask for the full room charge in writing before you accept an offer.</p>\n\n<h2>What is the cost of living in China for international students?</h2>\n<p>The cost of living in China for international students, excluding rent, is roughly CNY 1,000 to 1,400 a month on the university estimates we found, with big-city students more likely to spend CNY 1,800 to 2,500 once phone, transport and personal costs are added. Smaller cities are clearly cheaper than Shanghai or Beijing.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Monthly item (excluding dorm rent)</th><th>Tier-1 city (e.g. Shanghai, Beijing)</th><th>Tier-2 city (e.g. Qingdao, Zhenjiang, Dalian)</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>University''s own estimate</td><td>CNY 1,000&ndash;1,400 (Shanghai Jiao Tong University; meals alone CNY 600&ndash;900)</td><td>About CNY 1,200 (Jiangsu University)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Realistic planning budget</td><td>CNY 1,800&ndash;2,500 (&asymp; LKR 89,000&ndash;124,000)</td><td>CNY 1,200&ndash;1,800 (&asymp; LKR 60,000&ndash;89,000)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Books and materials</td><td colspan=\"2\">About CNY 1,000 a year (Jiangsu University estimate)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The \"realistic planning budget\" row is our own allowance on top of the official estimates, which tend to cover food and basics only. Living cost in Beijing or Shanghai for students rises sharply if a student rents privately instead of living on campus.</p>\n\n<h2>What one-time and yearly fees should Sri Lankan students budget for?</h2>\n<p>Beyond tuition and rent, a Sri Lankan student pays an application fee, a China visa fee in Colombo, a medical check in China, a residence permit and compulsory insurance. Together these come to roughly CNY 2,000 to 3,000 in the first year, plus flights.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Typical amount</th><th>Approx. LKR</th><th>Source example</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Application fee</td><td>CNY 200&ndash;800, usually non-refundable</td><td>&asymp; 10,000&ndash;40,000</td><td>Jiangsu University CNY 200; Fudan CNY 800</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Registration fee (some universities)</td><td>CNY 400</td><td>&asymp; 20,000</td><td>Jiangsu University</td></tr>\n<tr><td>China student visa (X1/X2), Sri Lankan passport</td><td>LKR 5,850 single entry</td><td>5,850</td><td>Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka, reduced-fee notice valid to 31 Dec 2026</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Medical check in China</td><td>CNY 200&ndash;600</td><td>&asymp; 10,000&ndash;30,000</td><td>China University of Petroleum; Shanghai Jiao Tong about CNY 500</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Residence permit</td><td>CNY 400 (under one year) or 800 (one to three years)</td><td>&asymp; 20,000&ndash;40,000</td><td>Beijing municipal government; varies by city</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Compulsory insurance, per year</td><td>CNY 600&ndash;800</td><td>&asymp; 30,000&ndash;40,000</td><td>Fudan and China University of Petroleum CNY 800; Jiangsu CNY 600</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Return flight Colombo&ndash;China</td><td>LKR 160,000&ndash;300,000</td><td>160,000&ndash;300,000</td><td>Airline fare pages, Oct 2026 (see note)</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>The CNY 800 a year insurance figure is common but not universal, so use your own university''s amount. Flight prices are airline \"from\" fares for Colombo to Shanghai return seen in October 2026 (Cathay Pacific from about LKR 160,000; Thai Airways about LKR 303,000 for October dates); they change daily and rise in the August&ndash;September travel season. Visa steps and documents are in our <a href=\"/guides/china-student-visa-sri-lanka\">China student visa guide</a>.</p>\n\n<h2>Worked example: a four-year bachelor''s degree</h2>\n<p>A self-funded four-year engineering bachelor''s degree at a tier-2 city university costs roughly LKR 10 million in total on the assumptions below. This is a hypothetical example built from the China University of Petroleum fees above, not a quotation.</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Cost item (4 years)</th><th>CNY</th><th>Approx. LKR</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td>Tuition: 20,000 &times; 4</td><td>80,000</td><td>3,976,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Twin dorm room: 650 &times; 12 months &times; 4</td><td>31,200</td><td>1,551,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Living costs: 1,500 &times; 12 months &times; 4</td><td>72,000</td><td>3,578,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insurance: 800 &times; 4</td><td>3,200</td><td>159,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Residence permit: 800 &times; 4</td><td>3,200</td><td>159,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Application fee and first-year medical check</td><td>1,400</td><td>70,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Visa (LKR 5,850) and four return flights (about LKR 200,000 each)</td><td>&ndash;</td><td>806,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Total</strong></td><td><strong>&asymp; 191,000 + flights</strong></td><td><strong>&asymp; LKR 10.3 million</strong></td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>That works out to about LKR 2.6 million a year. The same plan in Shanghai with higher tuition and living costs could easily reach LKR 13 to 14 million, while a Chinese-taught humanities programme in a smaller city would come in lower.</p>\n\n<h2>How do CSC and university scholarships change the total?</h2>\n<p>A full Chinese Government (CSC) scholarship pays tuition, a dormitory place and comprehensive insurance, and adds a monthly stipend of CNY 2,500 for undergraduates, CNY 3,000 for master''s students and CNY 3,500 for doctoral students. For most students the stipend covers daily living.</p>\n<p>In the worked example, a full CSC award removes tuition, dorm and insurance (about CNY 114,000) and pays CNY 120,000 in stipends over four years, more than the CNY 72,000 living budget. What the family still pays is mainly the application fee, visa, medical check, residence permit and flights: roughly LKR 1 to 1.2 million over four years instead of LKR 10.3 million. Partial university scholarships usually waive all or part of tuition only, so the family still pays rent and living costs.</p>\n<p>Competition for full awards is strong. See our <a href=\"/guides/chinese-government-scholarship-sri-lanka\">Chinese Government Scholarship guide</a> for how Sri Lankans apply, and the <a href=\"/guides/foreign-scholarships-for-sri-lankan-students\">foreign scholarships hub</a> to compare China with other countries.</p>\n\n<h2>How can Sri Lankan students reduce the cost of studying in China?</h2>\n<p>The biggest savings come from scholarships, choice of city and choice of teaching language. Small habits matter less than these three decisions.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Apply for scholarships every year you are eligible.</strong> CSC, provincial and university scholarships each have their own deadlines; check our <a href=\"/guides/china-university-intakes-and-deadlines\">intakes and deadlines guide</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Look beyond Shanghai and Beijing.</strong> Strong universities in Qingdao, Dalian, Harbin, Wuhan, Chengdu or Xi''an have lower rent and living costs.</li>\n<li><strong>Consider a Chinese-taught programme.</strong> Chinese-taught tuition is often lower, and a year of language study first can open those cheaper programmes; see our <a href=\"/guides/learn-chinese-hsk-sri-lanka\">HSK and learning Chinese guide</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Stay in the university dormitory</strong> at least in the first year, and eat in campus canteens.</li>\n<li><strong>Book flights early</strong> and avoid the late-August rush.</li>\n<li><strong>Pay fees directly to the university</strong> against an official invoice, and keep receipts.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h2>How Proton Education Services can help</h2>\n<p>Proton Education Services, with offices in Sri Lanka and Qingdao, helps Sri Lankan students compare universities by real fees, apply for CSC and university scholarships, and plan accommodation and arrival. See our <a href=\"/services/education\">study-in-China service</a> or <a href=\"/contact\">contact us</a> for a cost plan built around your subject and budget.</p>\n\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://cie.upc.edu.cn/admission_en/Admission/Tuition___Costs.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">China University of Petroleum (East China): Tuition &amp; Costs</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://study.tongji.edu.cn/en/CURRENT_STUDENTS/Free_Costs.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tongji University: International student fees and costs</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://oec.ujs.edu.cn/en/ADMISSIONS/Estimated_Costs.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jiangsu University: Estimated Costs</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://iec.dmu.edu.cn/info/1014/1447.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dalian Medical University: International education fees</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://iso.fudan.edu.cn/isoenglish/ResidencePermitforNewStudents/list.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fudan University: Residence Permit and insurance for new students</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://bjwb.seiee.sjtu.edu.cn/english/info/8374.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shanghai Jiao Tong University: Living Expense</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://en.sjtu.edu.cn/study-sjtu/current/services/74\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shanghai Jiao Tong University: Visa and Residence Permit</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://english.beijing.gov.cn/workinginbeijing/personalaffairsresidencepermits/itemsneedtoknow/202005/t20200520_1902704.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Beijing Municipal Government: Residence permit fees</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"http://lk.china-embassy.gov.cn/eng/zytz/202501/t20250102_11526013.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chinese Embassy in Sri Lanka: Extension of visa fee reduction</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://govt.chinadaily.com.cn/s/201803/16/WS5cd90c60498e079e68020317/chinese-government-scholarship-full-scholarship.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">China Daily (government portal): Chinese Government Scholarship full scholarship</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://flights.cathaypacific.com/destinations/en_HK/flights-from-colombo-to-chinese-mainland\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cathay Pacific: Flights from Colombo to Chinese Mainland</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.thaiairways.com/flights/en-lk/flights-from-colombo-to-shanghai\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Thai Airways: Flights from Colombo to Shanghai</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://currencyconvert.online/cny/lkr\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CurrencyConvert.online: CNY to LKR</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n</ul>","faq":[{"q":"How much does it cost to study in China for Sri Lankan students?","a":"For a self-funded bachelor''s degree, plan on roughly LKR 2 to 2.7 million a year, covering tuition of about CNY 17,000 to 25,000, a shared dorm room, insurance and living costs. A four-year degree in a tier-2 city comes to about LKR 10 million including flights. Medicine and Shanghai or Beijing universities cost more."},{"q":"Is studying in China cheaper than studying in the UK or Australia?","a":"Usually, yes. Chinese tuition for international bachelor''s students on the official pages we checked is about CNY 17,000 to 25,000 a year (around LKR 0.85 to 1.25 million), and dormitory rooms cost a few hundred yuan a month. Compare exact current figures for each country before deciding, because fees and exchange rates change."},{"q":"Can I study in China for free from Sri Lanka?","a":"Effectively yes, with a full Chinese Government (CSC) scholarship. It covers tuition, a dormitory place and insurance, and pays a monthly stipend of CNY 2,500 to 3,500 depending on level. You still pay your own flights, visa, medical check and residence permit, and competition for full awards is strong."},{"q":"How much money do I need per month in China as a student?","a":"Outside rent, university estimates put basic living costs at about CNY 1,000 to 1,400 a month. A safer planning figure is CNY 1,200 to 1,800 in cities such as Qingdao or Dalian and CNY 1,800 to 2,500 in Shanghai or Beijing, which is roughly LKR 60,000 to 124,000."},{"q":"Can international students work part-time in China to cover costs?","a":"Part-time work or internships need permission from your university and an endorsement on your residence permit from the local exit-entry authority. Rules and approval vary by city and university, so do not build your budget around part-time income. Ask your university''s international office before taking any job."}]}')), SortOrder = 9, UpdatedDate = GETDATE()
WHERE Slug = 'cost-of-studying-in-china-for-sri-lankans'
GO

UPDATE syst.Guide SET ContentJSON = JSON_MODIFY(ContentJSON, '$.en', JSON_QUERY(N'{"title":"MBBS in China for Sri Lankan Students: Fees, SLMC Rules and ERPM","seoTitle":"MBBS in China for Sri Lankan Students 2026 | Proton","description":"MBBS in China for Sri Lankan students: SLMC A/L rules, MOE-approved universities, fees in LKR, ERPM pass rates and red flags. Check before you enrol.","bodyHtml":"<p><strong>In short:</strong> MBBS in China for Sri Lankan students is a six-year, English-taught clinical medicine degree (five years of study plus a one-year internship) at a university the Chinese Ministry of Education has authorised to teach MBBS in English. To practise in Sri Lanka afterwards you must meet the Sri Lanka Medical Council (SLMC) entry rules before you enrol, get your degree approved by SLMC, and pass the ERPM licensing exam.</p><p>This guide covers who can apply, which universities are allowed to teach MBBS in English, realistic fees in rupees, what SLMC requires, and the warning signs to watch for. For the bigger picture of studying in China, start with our <a href=\"/guides/study-in-china-from-sri-lanka\">complete guide to studying in China from Sri Lanka</a>.</p><h2>Is MBBS in China valid in Sri Lanka?</h2><p>An MBBS from China can lead to medical registration in Sri Lanka, but only if three things line up: you met SLMC''s A/L entry standard when you started the course, the university was recognised by SLMC at the point you enrolled, and after graduating you obtain SLMC degree approval and pass the Examination for Registration to Practise Medicine (ERPM).</p><p>SLMC''s Overseas Graduates page says students \"should ensure that the university/medical school is recognised by SLMC at the point of registration to the course\". Without degree approval you cannot sit the ERPM, and without the ERPM you cannot get provisional registration or an internship slot through the Ministry of Health. SLMC links its list of recognised foreign degree programmes from the same page, so check the exact university and programme there before paying anything.</p><h3>SLMC entry requirements (A/L)</h3><p>For students who entered a foreign medical school on or after 10 April 2021, SLMC requires the following, in one sitting:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Qualification</th><th>SLMC minimum (Biology stream)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Sri Lankan GCE A/L</td><td>At least two Credit passes (C) and one Ordinary pass (S)</td></tr><tr><td>London / Cambridge / Edexcel A Level</td><td>At least two C grades and one D</td></tr></tbody></table><p>SLMC also states that pre-medical, foundation and preparatory courses are not accepted. A Maths-stream student, or a Biology-stream student below the SLMC minimum, may be admitted by a Chinese university but would not meet SLMC''s rule for registration in Sri Lanka. Example: a Biology-stream student with B, C, S in one sitting meets the minimum; a student with C, S, S does not.</p><h2>What is the ERPM and how hard is it?</h2><p>The ERPM is SLMC''s licensing exam for foreign medical graduates, previously called the \"Act 16 Examination\". A new ERPM Guideline was introduced in 2025, replacing the 2010 guideline. In a notice dated 26 May 2026, SLMC reported pass rates under the new format of 53% for Part I and 93% for Part II, an overall pass rate of 49%.</p><p>The same notice says SLMC has appointed an expert panel to review the 2025 guideline, so the ERPM format may change again before you graduate. SLMC''s homepage in October 2026 also lists an ERPM Part I / Part A and Part D timetable for October 2026, showing that older-format components are still being run for earlier candidates.</p><h3>Degree approval steps after graduating</h3><ol><li>Wait for SLMC to call for degree approval applications (for the 2027 ERPM the portal deadline is 27 November 2026, 4.00 p.m.).</li><li>Apply online and upload your final degree certificate, transcript with entry date and duration, and your original A/L certificate. Provisional pass certificates are not accepted.</li><li>Submit a university letter stating total onsite hospital hours in Medicine, Surgery, O&amp;G, Paediatrics, Psychiatry, Community Medicine and Forensic Medicine. Online teaching hours are excluded.</li><li>Provide copies of every passport used, including visa and entry stamp pages for the full training period.</li><li>Pay the degree approval fee (Rs. 13,200 as listed on the SLMC Overseas Graduates page, October 2026) and attend the verification interview in person.</li></ol><p>Steps 3 and 4 matter when choosing a university now: keep every passport, and pick a programme with real, documented hospital rotations, not online clinical teaching.</p><h2>Which Chinese universities can teach MBBS in English?</h2><p>Only universities named on China''s Ministry of Education (MOE) annual list may admit international students to English-taught clinical medicine. The MOE notice for 2023–2024 (教外厅函〔2023〕10号) listed 44 universities with a combined quota of 3,048 places, and stated that institutions not on the list may not enrol international students in English-taught clinical medicine or award an English-taught MBBS degree.</p><p>The MOE list is reviewed and can change each year, so do not rely on an agent''s printed list or an old brochure. Ask the university for its current MOE authorisation, and cross-check the same institution against SLMC''s recognised foreign degree programmes. A university must pass both checks.</p><h2>How long is MBBS in China?</h2><p>MBBS in China takes six years: five years of study (basic sciences, then clinical subjects) followed by a one-year clinical internship. Fudan University and Tongji University both list their English-taught MBBS as a six-year programme in 2026 admission material.</p><p>The MOE has stated that a bilingual (mixed English/Chinese) teaching model for MBBS is not allowed, so lectures should be in English. Chinese is still essential: from the clinical years onwards you will talk to patients and read hospital records in Chinese, and universities build Chinese language courses into the curriculum. Some universities set an HSK level before the internship year, so ask each university for its exact requirement. Our <a href=\"/guides/learn-chinese-hsk-sri-lanka\">HSK and learning Chinese guide</a> explains the levels and where to start in Sri Lanka.</p><h2>China MBBS fees for Sri Lankan students</h2><p>Tuition for English-taught MBBS in China ranges from about RMB 34,000 to RMB 75,000 a year on the university pages we checked, roughly LKR 1.7 to 3.7 million a year. Rupee figures below use CNY 1 ≈ LKR 49.7 (CurrencyConvert.online, 23 Oct 2026), rounded.</p><table><thead><tr><th>University</th><th>Tuition per year (RMB)</th><th>Approx. LKR per year</th><th>Source note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fudan University (Shanghai)</td><td>75,000</td><td>3.73 million</td><td>2026 programme list; \"for reference\", confirmed at registration</td></tr><tr><td>Xi''an Jiaotong University</td><td>40,000</td><td>1.99 million</td><td>Brochure for the Nov 2025 – Jun 2026 application window</td></tr><tr><td>Dalian Medical University</td><td>42,000</td><td>2.09 million</td><td>International college page; year not stated</td></tr><tr><td>Jiangsu University</td><td>34,000</td><td>1.69 million</td><td>Estimated costs page; year not stated</td></tr></tbody></table><p>These figures are tuition only. Confirm each fee on the university''s current admission notice before you apply, and check that the university is on both the current MOE list and SLMC''s recognised list.</p><h3>Example: total six-year cost</h3><p>Example (hypothetical): at a university charging RMB 40,000 a year, tuition alone over six years is RMB 240,000, about LKR 11.9 million. On top of that come accommodation, insurance, living costs, flights, visa and application fees (Fudan, for instance, charges a non-refundable RMB 800 application fee). Some universities reduce internship-year tuition and others do not, so ask. Our <a href=\"/guides/cost-of-studying-in-china-for-sri-lankans\">cost of studying in China guide</a> breaks down living costs by city.</p><h2>Are there scholarships for MBBS in China?</h2><p>Full scholarships for English-taught MBBS in China are rare. Most Chinese Government Scholarship (CSC) places go to other degrees, and universities seldom offer full MBBS awards of their own. Plan your budget assuming you will pay full fees, and treat any scholarship as a bonus.</p><p>If you want to try, read our <a href=\"/guides/chinese-government-scholarship-sri-lanka\">Chinese Government Scholarship guide</a> and watch the Sri Lanka Ministry of Education scholarship notices, which announce any CSC seats offered through the government-to-government channel.</p><h2>Red flags when choosing an MBBS agent or university</h2><ul><li>The university is not on the current MOE English-taught MBBS list, or the agent cannot show proof.</li><li>The agent says SLMC recognition \"doesn''t matter\" or can be \"sorted later\". SLMC expects it to be checked at the point you enrol.</li><li>You are offered admission without the SLMC A/L minimum, or through a \"foundation year\" to make up the grades. SLMC does not accept foundation courses.</li><li>Bilingual teaching, or online-only clinical rotations. SLMC excludes online hours.</li><li>Pressure to pay full tuition or a large \"seat fee\" to the agent instead of the university.</li><li>Guarantees of passing the ERPM or of a job in Sri Lanka. The overall new-format pass rate reported by SLMC was 49%.</li></ul><h2>Checklist before you enrol</h2><ol><li>Confirm your A/L results meet the SLMC Biology-stream minimum, in one sitting.</li><li>Check the university is on the current MOE English-taught MBBS list.</li><li>Check the same university and programme on SLMC''s recognised foreign degree programmes list, and save a dated screenshot.</li><li>Get the full fee schedule from the university in writing (tuition for all six years, internship year, accommodation, insurance).</li><li>Ask where the clinical rotations and internship happen and what Chinese level is required.</li><li>Prepare your documents early: see our <a href=\"/guides/documents-for-studying-in-china-sri-lanka\">documents and attestation guide</a>.</li><li>Keep every passport and visa page for all six years; SLMC asks for them.</li></ol><h2>How Proton Education Services can help</h2><p>Proton Education Services, with offices in Sri Lanka and Qingdao, helps students check university authorisation, compare fees, prepare applications and documents, and handle the X1 visa and arrival. We will always ask you to confirm SLMC recognition yourself before you pay. See our <a href=\"/services/education\">study-in-China service</a> or <a href=\"/contact\">contact us</a> to talk through your A/L results.</p><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://slmc.gov.lk/en/services/overseas-graduates\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sri Lanka Medical Council – Overseas Graduates (entry requirements, degree approval)</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://slmc.gov.lk/en\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sri Lanka Medical Council – homepage notices (2027 degree approval portal, October 2026 ERPM timetable)</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://slmc.gov.lk/public/guide/Notice_-_SLMC_Clarifies_Facts_Regarding_the_New_ERPM_Examination_Guideline__2025.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sri Lanka Medical Council – SLMC Clarifies Facts Regarding the New ERPM Examination Guideline 2025 (26 May 2026)</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://jw.beijing.gov.cn/xxgk/zfxxgkml/zfgkzcwj/202306/P020230602551804975291.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Ministry of Education of China (published by Beijing Municipal Education Commission) – 2023–2024 list of universities admitting international students to English-taught clinical medicine</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://cgishanghai.gov.in/Event?id=580\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Consulate General of India, Shanghai – Regarding medical courses offered by Chinese universities</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://iso.fudan.edu.cn/_upload/article/files/5f/66/4aee2d0c4b389ec2d922de9dc6d3/f3254ec3-a06d-43bb-8c5c-8b05daf9124b.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Fudan University – 2026 English-taught undergraduate programmes</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://sie.xjtu.edu.cn/en/YXBK.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Xi''an Jiaotong University – English-taught medical programme brochure</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://iec.dmu.edu.cn/info/1014/1447.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Dalian Medical University – International Education College MBBS page</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://oec.ujs.edu.cn/en/ADMISSIONS/Estimated_Costs.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jiangsu University – Estimated Costs</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li><a href=\"https://study.tongji.edu.cn/__local/3/E4/68/ECE8BA616EA392D20FC5176A6D0_5D68A87F_82DA8.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tongji University – international undergraduate recruitment plan</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li><li>CurrencyConvert.online – CNY to LKR rate, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li></ul>","faq":[{"q":"Can a Maths-stream student study MBBS in China and practise in Sri Lanka?","a":"A Chinese university may accept you, but SLMC requires students who entered a foreign medical school on or after 10 April 2021 to have Biology-stream A/Ls with at least two C passes and one S in one sitting. Without that, you would not meet SLMC''s rule for degree approval, so you could not sit the ERPM or register in Sri Lanka."},{"q":"How much does MBBS in China cost in total for a Sri Lankan student?","a":"Tuition alone at the universities we checked runs from about RMB 34,000 to 75,000 a year. At RMB 40,000 a year, six years of tuition comes to around LKR 11.9 million at October 2026 rates, before accommodation, insurance, living costs and flights. Always get the full six-year fee schedule in writing from the university."},{"q":"What is the ERPM pass rate?","a":"In a notice dated 26 May 2026, SLMC said the new-format ERPM had pass rates of 53% for Part I and 93% for Part II, giving an overall pass rate of 49%. SLMC has also set up an expert panel to review the 2025 guideline, so the format could change before current students graduate."},{"q":"Do I need HSK for MBBS in China?","a":"Lectures in an authorised English-taught MBBS are in English, but you need working Chinese for clinical rotations and the internship, where patients and records are in Chinese. Universities include Chinese classes in the programme and some set an HSK level before the internship, so ask each university for its exact rule."},{"q":"Where can I check if a Chinese medical university is SLMC approved?","a":"SLMC''s Overseas Graduates page on slmc.gov.lk links to its list of recognised foreign degree programmes. Check the exact university and programme there before you enrol, save a dated copy, and also confirm the university appears on China''s current Ministry of Education list for English-taught MBBS."}]}')), SortOrder = 10, UpdatedDate = GETDATE()
WHERE Slug = 'mbbs-in-china-sri-lanka'
GO

UPDATE syst.Guide SET ContentJSON = JSON_MODIFY(ContentJSON, '$.en', JSON_QUERY(N'{"title":"HSK Exam in Sri Lanka 2026–27: Dates, Fees, Test Centres & Levels","seoTitle":"HSK Exam Sri Lanka 2026–27: Dates, Fees & Centres | Proton","description":"HSK exam in Sri Lanka explained: Kelaniya and Colombo test centres, 2026–27 dates, fees in LKR, how to register and which level you need for China.","bodyHtml":"<p><strong>In short:</strong> You can sit the HSK exam in Sri Lanka at two official test centres, the Confucius Institute at the University of Kelaniya and the Confucius Institute at the University of Colombo. Both run paper-based sessions during the year. You register online at chinesetest.cn and pay the fee in rupees to the centre. Most Sri Lankans need HSK 3 to HSK 5, depending on whether they want a Chinese-taught degree, a scholarship or clinical training in China.</p><p>This guide covers the HSK exam in Sri Lanka for 2026–27: what the levels mean, where and when to sit the test, what it costs, how to register, and where to learn Chinese before you go. For the bigger picture of studying in China, start with our <a href=\"/guides/study-in-china-from-sri-lanka\">complete guide to studying in China from Sri Lanka</a>.</p><h2>What is the HSK exam?</h2><p>The HSK (Hanyu Shuiping Kaoshi) is China''s official Chinese proficiency test for non-native speakers, and registration for it runs through chinesetest.cn. Chinese universities and the Chinese Government Scholarship use HSK results to decide whether a student can follow lectures taught in Chinese.</p><p>A separate speaking test, the <strong>HSKK</strong>, comes in Primary, Intermediate and Advanced levels. The Colombo test centre pairs HSK 3 with HSKK Primary, HSK 4 with HSKK Intermediate, and HSK 5–6 with HSKK Advanced. Some scholarships ask for an HSKK score as well as the HSK.</p><h3>HSK 2.0 (six levels) or the new HSK 3.0 (nine levels)?</h3><p>A new \"three stages, nine levels\" HSK framework (often called HSK 3.0) has been published: Levels 1–3 elementary, 4–6 intermediate and 7–9 advanced. The advanced HSK 7–9 already runs as a single exam. However, the sources we checked disagree on when the new format replaces the six-level test for Levels 1–6, and at least one 2026 update says regular sessions still use the six-level format until a formal launch is announced.</p><p>The practical advice: when you register for the HSK exam in Sri Lanka, check on chinesetest.cn which version and syllabus your session uses, and study the matching word list. Universities and the CSC currently write their requirements in terms of the six-level HSK.</p><h2>Where can I take the HSK exam in Sri Lanka?</h2><p>Sri Lanka has two established HSK test centres, both Confucius Institutes attached to state universities. Both register candidates through chinesetest.cn and are open to the public, not only to their own students.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Test centre</th><th>Location</th><th>Recent sessions</th><th>Contact</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Confucius Institute, University of Kelaniya</td><td>Kelaniya (Dalugama)</td><td>24 January 2026 and 28 June 2026</td><td><a href=\"https://units.kln.ac.lk/ci/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">units.kln.ac.lk/ci</a></td></tr><tr><td>Confucius Institute, University of Colombo</td><td>Faculty of Arts, Colombo 03 (116A, 1st floor)</td><td>Onsite HSK/HSKK on 7 November 2026</td><td>hsk@ciuc.cmb.ac.lk, 011 259 9869</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Confucius Classrooms in schools and other institutions teach Chinese but are not listed as HSK test venues. If you live outside the Western Province, plan for a trip to Kelaniya or Colombo on test day.</p><h2>When are the HSK exam dates for 2026 and 2027 in Sri Lanka?</h2><p>HSK exam dates in Sri Lanka are chosen by each test centre from the global calendar on chinesetest.cn. In 2026 the Kelaniya centre held sessions in January and June, and the Colombo centre scheduled an onsite session on 7 November 2026 with a registration deadline of 17 September 2026, so registration for that sitting has closed.</p><p>The 2027 dates for Sri Lankan centres had not been published when this guide was written (23 October 2026). To find them:</p><ol><li>Log in to chinesetest.cn, open test registration, select Sri Lanka and see which dates each centre has opened.</li><li>Watch the news pages of the Kelaniya and Colombo Confucius Institutes, where each session is announced.</li><li>Expect registration to close well before the test: Colombo''s November 2026 deadline was about seven weeks ahead.</li></ol><p>If you are aiming for a September 2027 university intake, sit the HSK by early 2027 so the score report is ready when applications open. Our <a href=\"/guides/china-university-intakes-and-deadlines\">intakes and deadlines guide</a> shows when each application window opens.</p><h2>How much does the HSK exam cost in Sri Lanka?</h2><p>HSK exam fees in Sri Lanka are set by each test centre and paid in rupees by bank deposit. The most recent public fee schedule we found is the University of Kelaniya Confucius Institute''s 2024 \"HSK/HSKK test fee standard\", so treat these figures as a guide: the 2026–27 fees may be different.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Item (Kelaniya, 2024 schedule)</th><th>Fee</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>HSK 1, outside candidate</td><td>LKR 2,000</td></tr><tr><td>HSK 1, University of Kelaniya student</td><td>LKR 1,800</td></tr><tr><td>HSK 3 + HSKK combined</td><td>LKR 7,200</td></tr><tr><td>HSK 6 + HSKK combined</td><td>LKR 14,400</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Fees rise with each level. Kelaniya asks candidates to pay into the institute''s People''s Bank (Dalugama branch) account and write their name and test level on the deposit slip. The Colombo centre warns that a fee paid into the wrong account is not refunded. Always confirm the current fee and account details in the centre''s notice for your session before paying.</p><h2>How do I register for the HSK exam in Sri Lanka?</h2><p>Registration for the HSK exam in Sri Lanka is online at chinesetest.cn, followed by payment to your chosen test centre before its deadline.</p><ol><li>Create an account on chinesetest.cn using your name exactly as it appears in your passport or NIC.</li><li>Choose the test (HSK, HSKK or both), the level, Sri Lanka as the country and Kelaniya or Colombo as the centre, then select an open date.</li><li>Upload a recent passport-style photo and enter your ID details.</li><li>Pay the fee to the bank account given in the centre''s notice, keep the deposit slip, and follow the centre''s instructions for sending proof of payment.</li><li>About ten days before the exam, log in to chinesetest.cn and print your admission ticket.</li><li>On test day, bring the admission ticket and the same ID you registered with.</li><li>After results are released, download your score report from chinesetest.cn; the printed certificate reaches the test centre later.</li></ol><h2>Can I take the HSK exam online from home?</h2><p>An HSK home-based online test (\"Home Edition\") exists, but it was introduced for countries where on-site testing had not resumed. Because both Sri Lankan centres now run on-site sessions, most Sri Lankans should expect to sit the HSK at a centre.</p><p>When the Colombo Confucius Institute ran online tests in 2021, the home edition had strict requirements: a Windows PC (no Mac or mobile), a camera and a stable connection, and some centres overseas charged about 30% more than the on-site fee. Ask the test centre whether a home edition is offered for any 2027 date before planning around it.</p><h2>Which HSK level do I need to study in China?</h2><p>The HSK level you need depends on the language of your programme. English-taught degrees usually need no HSK at all. For Chinese-taught degrees, Chinese Government Scholarship criteria set HSK 3 for undergraduate study and HSK 4 for master''s and PhD, and individual universities can ask for more.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Typical HSK requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>English-taught bachelor''s or master''s</td><td>None (Chinese still helps with daily life)</td></tr><tr><td>Chinese-taught bachelor''s (CSC criteria)</td><td>HSK 3; some universities ask for HSK 4</td></tr><tr><td>Chinese-taught master''s or PhD (CSC criteria)</td><td>HSK 4 or higher</td></tr><tr><td>Chinese language programme</td><td>None needed to start</td></tr><tr><td>International Chinese Language Teachers Scholarship (CIS), one-year Chinese study (2026 guides)</td><td>HSK 3 with at least 210; an HSKK score gives priority</td></tr><tr><td>CIS, one-year teaching Chinese track (2026 guides)</td><td>HSK 3 with at least 270, plus an HSKK score</td></tr><tr><td>English-taught MBBS, before clinical internship in China</td><td>Usually HSK 4 (e.g. 180+ at Jiangsu University); some universities ask for more</td></tr></tbody></table><p>For MBBS students, Chinese matters even on an English-taught programme. University regulations, and an advisory by the Indian Consulate in Guangzhou citing China''s Ministry of Education, state that students must reach about HSK 4 before hospital rotations, and a student who does not meet the language requirement may not be awarded the degree. Read more in our <a href=\"/guides/mbbs-in-china-sri-lanka\">MBBS in China guide</a>, and see the <a href=\"/guides/chinese-government-scholarship-sri-lanka\">Chinese Government Scholarship guide</a> for how HSK fits into a CSC application. For awards from other countries, see the <a href=\"/guides/foreign-scholarships-for-sri-lankan-students\">foreign scholarships hub</a>.</p><h2>Where can I learn Chinese in Sri Lanka?</h2><p>The main places to learn Chinese in Sri Lanka are the two Confucius Institutes, which run part-time courses for the public, along with university degree courses and online tutors. Progress depends on hours of study, so choose a course that maps to a specific HSK level.</p><ul><li><strong>Confucius Institute, University of Colombo:</strong> takes applications for public Chinese courses in batches (a \"2026 1st batch\" call was published), taught at the Faculty of Arts.</li><li><strong>Confucius Institute, University of Kelaniya:</strong> has advertised weekend Chinese courses for the public; check the institute''s site for the next intake.</li><li><strong>Online:</strong> self-study apps plus a tutor work well for HSK 1–3; follow the official HSK word list for your level.</li><li><strong>In China:</strong> a one-semester or one-year Chinese language programme at a Chinese university before your degree, often the fastest way to reach HSK 4.</li></ul><h2>HSK and the CSCA Chinese papers</h2><p>Many Chinese universities now ask international undergraduate applicants to sit the CSCA (China Scholastic Competency Assessment). Applicants to Chinese-taught degrees take a CSCA Chinese paper (STEM Chinese or Humanities Chinese) that tests academic Chinese at first-year university level, which is different from the general language tested by the HSK, so plan for both. Our <a href=\"/guides/csca-exam-sri-lanka\">CSCA exam guide</a> explains the papers and who needs them.</p><h2>How Proton Education Services can help</h2><p>Proton Education Services advises Sri Lankan students on which HSK level their chosen university and scholarship need, and runs online 3-month CSCA STEM Chinese and Humanities Chinese courses (48 hours each, LKR 40,000). We also handle university and CSC scholarship applications and visa support. See our <a href=\"/services/education\">study-in-China services</a> or <a href=\"/contact\">contact us</a>.</p><h2>Sources</h2><ul><li><a href=\"https://www.chinesetest.cn/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chinese Testing International – chinesetest.cn HSK registration</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://units.kln.ac.lk/ci/media/attachments/2024/02/09/hsk-hskk-test-fee-standard.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Confucius Institute, University of Kelaniya – HSK/HSKK test fee standard (2024)</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://units.kln.ac.lk/ci/index.php/event/news/231-confucius-institute-at-the-university-of-kelaniya-successfully-conducts-hsk-2026-examination\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Confucius Institute, University of Kelaniya – HSK 2026 examination news</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://arts.cmb.ac.lk/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/1、About-Onsite-Chinese-Test-2026.11.7.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">University of Colombo, Faculty of Arts – About Onsite Chinese Test 2026.11.7</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://cmb.ac.lk/wp-content/uploads/notice-onsite-hsk-hskk-chinese-test-instruction.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">University of Colombo – Onsite HSK/HSKK test instructions</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://ciuc.cmb.ac.lk/wp-content/uploads/2021/08/About-Online-Chinese-Test-2021.10.30-1.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Confucius Institute, University of Colombo – About Online Chinese Test (2021)</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://arts.cmb.ac.lk/wp-content/uploads/2026/02/Chinese-Courses-Application-2026-1st-batch.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">University of Colombo, Faculty of Arts – Chinese Courses Application 2026 1st batch</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://sheffield.ac.uk/confucius/proficiency-tests/hsk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">University of Sheffield Confucius Institute – HSK 2.0 and HSK 3.0 levels</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://studycli.org/hsk/the-new-hsk\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">StudyCLI – The newly revised HSK (2026)</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://www.southampton.ac.uk/~assets/doc/ci/HSK_Language_tests/Notice%20on%20Additional%20HSK,%20HSKK,%20and%20YCT%20Online%20Chinese%20Test%20(Home%20Edition)%20in%202023.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">University of Southampton Confucius Institute – Online Chinese Test (Home Edition) 2023 notice</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://eniec.cug.edu.cn/Scholarships/Chinese_Government_Scholarships.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">China University of Geosciences – Chinese Government Scholarships (HSK criteria)</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://ies-en.zuel.edu.cn/2026/0317/c13715a424431/page.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Zhongnan University of Economics and Law – International Chinese Language Teachers Scholarship 2026 guide</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://oec.ujs.edu.cn/info/1516/3487.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Jiangsu University – MBBS internship requirements</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li><li><a href=\"https://cgiguangzhou.gov.in/docs/Advisory%20for%20Indian%20Students.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Consulate General of India, Guangzhou – Advisory for Indian students on medical study in China</a> (accessed 23 Oct 2026)</li></ul>","faq":[{"q":"Is the HSK exam held in Sri Lanka?","a":"Yes. The Confucius Institutes at the University of Kelaniya and the University of Colombo are HSK test centres and run on-site HSK and HSKK sessions during the year. You register online at chinesetest.cn, choose one of the two centres and an open date, then pay the fee in rupees by bank deposit following that centre''s notice."},{"q":"Can I study in China without knowing Chinese?","a":"Yes. Many Chinese universities offer English-taught bachelor''s and master''s degrees, including MBBS, that need no HSK for admission. You will still need basic Chinese for daily life, and MBBS students usually must reach around HSK 4 before hospital rotations, so starting Chinese classes before you leave Sri Lanka is a sensible plan."},{"q":"Do I need HSK for the Chinese Government Scholarship?","a":"Only for Chinese-taught programmes. CSC criteria ask for HSK 3 for Chinese-taught undergraduate study and HSK 4 for Chinese-taught master''s or PhD, and universities may set higher levels. Applicants to English-taught programmes or to a Chinese language course do not need an HSK score, although one can strengthen an application."},{"q":"Should I take the HSKK speaking test as well?","a":"Take the HSKK if your scholarship or university asks for it, or if you want a stronger application. The 2026 International Chinese Language Teachers Scholarship guides require or give priority to an HSKK score. In Sri Lanka you can usually sit the HSKK on the same day as the HSK, and combined fees are listed by each centre."},{"q":"What if I miss the HSK registration deadline in Sri Lanka?","a":"Late registration is generally not possible once a centre closes a session on chinesetest.cn. Check whether the other Sri Lankan centre has a later date open, or plan for the next session. If a university deadline is close, ask the university whether it will accept your HSK score after the application, as some do."}]}')), SortOrder = 11, UpdatedDate = GETDATE()
WHERE Slug = 'learn-chinese-hsk-sri-lanka'
GO

UPDATE syst.Guide SET ContentJSON = JSON_MODIFY(ContentJSON, '$.en', JSON_QUERY(N'{"title":"What to Do After A/L in Sri Lanka: Every Option Compared","seoTitle":"What to Do After A/L in Sri Lanka: Your Options | Proton","description":"What to do after A/L in Sri Lanka? Compare state universities, private and external degrees, CIMA/CA and study abroad by cost, time and recognition.","bodyHtml":"<p><strong>In short:</strong> if you are wondering what to do after A/L in Sri Lanka, you have five realistic routes: a state university through the UGC, a UGC-recognised non-state degree, an external degree, a vocational or professional qualification, or a degree abroad. Only about one in four students who qualify for university entrance is actually selected for a state university, so most families need to compare the other routes seriously.</p>\n<p>This guide explains how state university selection works, compares every route on cost, time and recognition, maps study-abroad options in China to each A/L stream, and ends with a checklist to help you decide.</p>\n<h2>Didn''t get into a state university? Your options</h2>\n<p>Not being selected for a state university is the normal outcome, not a failure. In the 2023/2024 intake, 173,541 candidates qualified for university admission but 44,852 were admitted to state universities and higher educational institutes, according to the University Grants Commission (UGC). That is about 25.6% of qualified students.</p>\n<p>If your Z-score did not reach the cut-off for the course you wanted, your main choices are to sit the A/L again, enrol at a recognised non-state institute, take an external degree, start a professional or vocational qualification, or study abroad. Each route suits a different budget, timeline and career goal, which the comparison table below sets out.</p>\n<h2>How does state university admission work in Sri Lanka?</h2>\n<p>State university admission is managed centrally by the UGC. You must first qualify for university admission through your A/L results, then apply online to the UGC, which ranks applicants by Z-score for each course and district and selects students up to the number of places available. Cut-off Z-scores change every year.</p>\n<p>The Z-score adjusts your raw marks for how difficult each subject paper was, so students taking different subjects can be compared. Most places are filled on district quotas, so the cut-off for the same course can differ between Colombo and other districts. The UGC publishes the minimum Z-scores after selection each year on <a href=\"https://www.ugc.ac.lk/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ugc.ac.lk</a>.</p>\n<p>Selection odds also depend heavily on your stream. The UGC''s figures for the 2023/2024 intake (based on the 2023 A/L) show:</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>A/L stream</th><th>Qualified</th><th>Admitted</th><th>Admitted (%)</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Arts</td><td>65,811</td><td>13,362</td><td>20.3%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Commerce</td><td>39,041</td><td>8,289</td><td>21.2%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Physical Science (Maths)</td><td>20,478</td><td>7,929</td><td>38.7%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Biological Science</td><td>27,586</td><td>10,473</td><td>38.0%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Engineering Technology</td><td>11,604</td><td>2,441</td><td>21.0%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Biosystems Technology</td><td>6,239</td><td>1,578</td><td>25.3%</td></tr>\n<tr><td>All streams (incl. other combinations)</td><td>173,541</td><td>44,852</td><td>25.6%</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Within the Bio stream, competition for medicine is far tighter than these averages suggest, because medicine has a limited number of places and among the highest cut-offs.</p>\n<h2>After A/L courses in Sri Lanka and abroad: how do the routes compare?</h2>\n<p>The five routes after A/L differ mainly in cost, how long they take, and who recognises the qualification. The table gives a general comparison. Fees vary widely between institutes and change often, so always ask for a written total cost before you enrol.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Typical cost</th><th>Time to qualify</th><th>Recognition</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>State university (UGC)</td><td>Tuition-free; you pay living costs</td><td>Usually 3–4 years (medicine longer), plus any wait before the intake starts</td><td>Fully recognised in Sri Lanka</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Non-state degree-awarding institute (UGC-recognised)</td><td>Private tuition fees; varies widely by institute and course</td><td>Usually 3–4 years</td><td>Recognised only if the institute and the specific degree are on the approved list</td></tr>\n<tr><td>External degree (a Sri Lankan state university''s external programme or a foreign university''s external route)</td><td>Usually lower than private full-time degrees</td><td>3 years or more, depending on your own pace</td><td>Degree is awarded by the university itself; employer perception varies</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Vocational / NVQ / professional (CIMA, CA Sri Lanka, ACCA, etc.)</td><td>Paid per level or per exam</td><td>Flexible; you can work while studying</td><td>Strong in the relevant industry; NVQ levels are nationally recognised</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Study abroad (e.g. China)</td><td>From near-free with a full scholarship to a significant self-funded cost</td><td>Usually 4 years for a bachelor''s; MBBS longer</td><td>Depends on the university and country; check with the relevant Sri Lankan body (e.g. the SLMC for medicine)</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>Before paying for a private degree in Sri Lanka, check the UGC website for the current list of recognised non-state higher education institutes and the specific degree programmes approved for each. Recognition applies to programmes, not just to the institute name.</p>\n<h2>What are the best after A/L degree courses in Sri Lanka without a state university place?</h2>\n<p>The best after A/L degree course is the one that leads to the career you want and is recognised where you plan to work. Professional qualifications such as CIMA or CA Sri Lanka suit Commerce students who want to start earning early, while a recognised degree suits careers that require one for entry or promotion.</p>\n<p>Many students combine routes: for example, starting a professional qualification while waiting to see whether a second A/L attempt or a scholarship application succeeds. Plan the combination so the timelines do not clash.</p>\n<h2>Study abroad after A levels: what can each A/L stream study in China?</h2>\n<p>Chinese universities accept Sri Lankan A/L qualifications for bachelor''s programmes, with English-taught and Chinese-taught options. For 2026 and 2027 entry, undergraduate applicants to Chinese Government Scholarship universities must sit the China Scholastic Competency Assessment (CSCA), and many other universities also ask for it.</p>\n<table><thead><tr><th>A/L stream</th><th>Common bachelor''s choices in China</th><th>CSCA subjects to plan for</th></tr></thead><tbody>\n<tr><td>Biological Science</td><td>MBBS (clinical medicine), nursing, pharmacy, biotechnology</td><td>Mathematics plus Chemistry and/or Physics</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Physical Science (Maths)</td><td>Engineering, computer science, data science, architecture</td><td>Mathematics plus Physics and/or Chemistry</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Commerce</td><td>Business administration, international trade, economics, finance</td><td>Mathematics</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arts</td><td>Chinese language, international relations, education, other humanities</td><td>Mathematics; Humanities Professional Chinese for Chinese-taught programmes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Technology (Engineering / Biosystems)</td><td>Applied engineering, IT, agriculture, food science</td><td>Mathematics plus Physics and/or Chemistry</td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p>The CSCA has four subjects: Professional Chinese (Humanities or STEM category), Mathematics, Physics and Chemistry. According to the Shanghai government''s CSCA FAQ, Mathematics is compulsory for all programmes; science, engineering, agriculture and medicine applicants must also take at least one of Physics or Chemistry; and applicants to fully English-taught programmes are exempt from Professional Chinese. Each university sets its exact requirements, so check the programme page. Our <a href=\"/guides/csca-exam-sri-lanka\">CSCA exam guide for Sri Lankan students</a> explains the exam in detail.</p>\n<p>Medicine needs extra care: before choosing MBBS abroad, confirm that the university and programme meet the Sri Lanka Medical Council''s current requirements for registration. The <a href=\"/guides/mbbs-in-china-sri-lanka\">MBBS in China guide</a> covers this.</p>\n<h2>Are there scholarships for Sri Lankan students after A/L?</h2>\n<p>Yes. The Chinese Government Scholarship (CSC) is the best-known option for China and, depending on the category, can cover tuition, accommodation and a monthly stipend. Other countries offer government and university scholarships too, and some are announced in Sri Lanka through the Ministry of Education.</p>\n<p>Read the <a href=\"/guides/chinese-government-scholarship-sri-lanka\">Chinese Government Scholarship guide</a> for eligibility and application steps, and the <a href=\"/guides/foreign-scholarships-for-sri-lankan-students\">foreign scholarships hub</a> for options in other countries. For self-funded budgets, see the <a href=\"/guides/cost-of-studying-in-china-for-sri-lankans\">cost of studying in China for Sri Lankans</a>.</p>\n<h2>When should you plan: results, UGC applications and intakes?</h2>\n<p>Start planning before your A/L results arrive. The 2025 A/L results were released on 31 March 2026, and the UGC application and selection process follows over the next several months. Chinese universities mainly admit in September, with application deadlines often in the first half of the year.</p>\n<p>Because a state university intake can start long after results, many students apply abroad in parallel. A practical timeline for a September intake in China:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Choose a stream-matched programme and check its CSCA and language requirements.</li>\n<li>Register for and sit a CSCA session that delivers your score before the university''s deadline (sessions are held in January, March, April, June and December).</li>\n<li>Prepare documents and apply for scholarships and admission, usually between January and spring.</li>\n<li>After admission, apply for the X1 visa and arrange travel for September.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Exact deadlines differ by university; see <a href=\"/guides/china-university-intakes-and-deadlines\">China university intakes and deadlines</a> and <a href=\"/guides/csca-exam-registration\">how to register for the CSCA</a>.</p>\n<h2>How to decide what to do after A/L: a checklist</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Career:</strong> which job do you want, and what qualification does it actually require?</li>\n<li><strong>Odds:</strong> how close was your Z-score to last year''s cut-off for your course and district? Is a second attempt realistic?</li>\n<li><strong>Budget:</strong> what is the full cost, including living expenses and travel, and could a scholarship cover part of it?</li>\n<li><strong>Recognition:</strong> is the degree recognised by the UGC, the relevant professional body or the employers you want?</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> how many years until you graduate, including waiting periods?</li>\n<li><strong>Language and independence:</strong> are you ready to live abroad and learn a new language such as Chinese?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Example: a Maths-stream student whose Z-score fell just short of the engineering cut-off might sit the CSCA Mathematics and Physics papers, apply for an English-taught engineering degree in China with a CSC scholarship, and keep a second A/L attempt as a backup. For the full picture, read our <a href=\"/guides/study-in-china-from-sri-lanka\">complete guide to studying in China from Sri Lanka</a>.</p>\n<h2>How Proton Education Services can help</h2>\n<p>Proton Education Services is a Sri Lankan consultancy with offices in Sri Lanka and Qingdao, China. We help A/L students choose a stream-matched programme, prepare for the CSCA with online courses, apply for CSC and university scholarships, and handle the visa and arrival. See our <a href=\"/services/education\">study in China service</a> or <a href=\"/contact\">contact us</a> to talk through your results.</p>\n<h2>Sources</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ugc.ac.lk/downloads/statistics/stat_2024/Chapter%202.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">University Grants Commission Sri Lanka, Statistics 2024, Chapter 2: University Admission (Table 02-01)</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://www.ugc.ac.lk/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">University Grants Commission Sri Lanka, home page</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://english.shanghai.gov.cn/en-FAQs-StudyinShanghai/20251218/134bb78943f640519e2185ff83051f5d.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Shanghai Municipal Government, China Scholastic Competency Assessment: FAQs</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n<li><a href=\"https://english.newsfirst.lk/2026/04/01/over-62-64-qualify-for-university-admission-as-2025-a-l-results-are-released\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">News 1st, 2025 A/L results released</a>, accessed 23 Oct 2026</li>\n</ul>","faq":[{"q":"What can I do after A/L if I am not selected for a state university?","a":"You can sit the A/L again, enrol in a UGC-recognised non-state degree, take an external degree, start a professional qualification such as CIMA or CA Sri Lanka, or study abroad. Studying in China is a popular option because Chinese universities accept A/L results, offer English-taught programmes and run scholarships such as the CSC."},{"q":"How many students get into state universities in Sri Lanka?","a":"For the 2023/2024 intake, the UGC reports 173,541 students qualified for university admission and 44,852 were admitted, roughly one in four. Selection rates differ by stream: about 38% in the Bio and Maths streams and around 20 to 21% in Arts and Commerce."},{"q":"Can I study abroad after A/L with average results?","a":"Often yes. Foreign universities set their own entry requirements, and Chinese universities now also look at CSCA scores for undergraduate entry. Competitive programmes and full scholarships still need strong results, so compare several universities and check each programme''s minimum grades before applying."},{"q":"Do I need the CSCA to study in China after A/L?","a":"For 2026 and 2027 entry, the CSCA is required for undergraduate applicants to Chinese Government Scholarship universities, and many other universities ask for it too. Mathematics is compulsory; science and medicine applicants also take Physics or Chemistry. English-taught applicants are exempt from Professional Chinese."},{"q":"Is a private degree in Sri Lanka recognised?","a":"Only if the institute and the specific degree programme are approved. Check the UGC website for the current list of recognised non-state degree-awarding institutes and their approved programmes before you pay any fees, and ask the institute for its approval details in writing."}]}')), SortOrder = 12, UpdatedDate = GETDATE()
WHERE Slug = 'what-to-do-after-al-sri-lanka'
GO
